window.DLI_SCRIPT=[{"id":"cover","index":0,"title":"SO-101 Sim-to-Real","steps":[{"id":"cover:cue:0","title":"The workflow","html":"<p>Our question for the day is simple: why can a motion learned in simulation behave differently on a real robot? Placing one small vial in a rack brings together perception, joint control, contact, and learning data. We will collect demonstrations, evaluate prepared policies, and learn how to turn a failure into a useful next experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"cover","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"55da6c68ef635edb2d36c5cff275fcbcf292dc704339c20108642a33774e3dc1","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"cover:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>First, connect this question to the tools and objectives of today’s experiments.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"cover","kind":"slide","index":1}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-slide","page":"index.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"d75d4295e1597c7cab05679653f2d3af76a55f1d7bb8d93e97eea5f1858fc9e9","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--index--1","index":1,"title":"Train an SO-101 Robot From Sim-to-Real With NVIDIA Isaac","steps":[{"id":"topic--index--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to connect device preparation, data collection and review, GR00T training, and simulation and real-world evaluation into one workflow. We will compare which gaps the five Sim-to-Real strategies address, starting with the overall exercise sequence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--index--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"index.html"}},{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:0","title":"What connects the workflow","html":"<p>Let us place each tool in that experiment. Isaac Lab organizes the environment and its observation–action loop; this workshop uses Newton for physics and ovrtx for camera rendering. LeRobot connects devices, datasets, training, and inference, while GR00T predicts actions from observations. These boundaries give us a practical way to locate a mismatch before changing the model.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"overview","source_sha256":"c66385bcb575a442003d7759ac408e5f2d57ea4a279dcf8ee27d23665802b1f3","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:7","title":"The exercise sequence","html":"<p>We first align the arms and cameras, then collect simulation demonstrations with varied conditions. Real evaluation will guide additions of real data, corrections, video augmentation, and physics calibration. Prepared checkpoints let us compare behavior without waiting for a complete training run.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Image 1 · Robot Calibration","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"overview","text":"Robot Calibration"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"overview","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"overview","source_sha256":"920de4dfc46a54427f91f48a31d275399dea57cbaf1fa64ea92f303641ed4ed5"}},{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Putting this workflow into the schedule clarifies what we will practice and what we will compare.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","kind":"slide","index":2}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"index.html","anchor":"train-an-so-101-robot-from-sim-to-real-with-nvidia-isaac","source_sha256":"e5fb85d94f66cc0d998b0ff0bd94a86fff56df2c780bd4af04c990b399e85cca","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"agenda","index":2,"title":"Today’s Schedule","steps":[{"id":"agenda:cue:0","title":"The day ahead","html":"<p>In the morning we establish reliable robot and camera inputs, examine how demonstrations become GR00T training examples, and evaluate a policy in simulation. In the afternoon we take that policy to hardware and decide whether a failure calls for real data, human corrections, visual augmentation, or a better physical model. Explanations and demonstrations will be followed by time to practice and review the results together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"agenda","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"386cc16bcb6b5e21cf55e413e55de34659896f4517e99842c416993b44990b3c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"agenda:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Our first concept is the Physical AI loop: an action’s consequence becomes part of the next input.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"agenda","kind":"slide","index":3}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"6865cc4bbd28a970c75170af130e9b2fedaf71b06c32daefffbeaf535e0eb2c4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1","index":3,"title":"Physical AI and Robot Learning","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:0","title":"Observe and act","html":"<p>A robot’s prediction changes its next input. If the gripper nudges a vial, the object moves and the next camera image changes with it. This observation–action–observation loop is our starting point for Physical AI. Recognizing a scene correctly is useful; completing a task also requires responding to the consequences of previous actions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"a44712a05b84608b7a19d3d0b97c4af3d08baff08e93d91cdabf694f34809801","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:9","title":"From goal to action","html":"<p>In this loop, language specifies the goal of placing a vial in the rack. GR00T receives wrist and external images plus current joint positions and predicts an action sequence. After execution, new images and joint feedback inform the next decision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Describe the task","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"kind":"diagram","term":"instruction"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"3bc1e23eefb90c6413a474a6cf56f0b68f41d97e6868a5af6accc5aed2c1fada"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:observe","title":"Observe the current scene","html":"<p>Two RGB views expose only part of the physical state: the vial tip may be occluded and contact force may not be visible. Joint positions add the robot’s own pose. Each new observation updates our evidence about the scene changed by the previous action.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"observe"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"observe","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"637e90d2a2b1f80286ecb1a282f1d1e0adf02d6239a138c23d43cfa409939be2"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:predict","title":"Predict actions","html":"<p>The policy generates an action sequence based on what it learned about suitable actions for the observation. The instruction conditions the goal; it does not guarantee a new skill on demand. Checkpoint weights remain fixed during inference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"predict"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"predict","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"ef347d676ddecc44692df4d8643fc2d4714c583664be19590d917dd995602473"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:act","title":"Act, then observe again","html":"<p>A command is a target; actual motion is the response of the device and environment. Friction and contact can make the two differ, so we observe the outcome again. That feedback distinguishes policy execution from simply replaying a fixed trajectory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"act"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"act","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"d5f5fdcad2e9adcf14b4d26feb011ba0cfc8341eb8536658b92005cc80f9066d"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:12","title":"Task breadth and proficiency","html":"<p>The range of tasks handled by this loop distinguishes specialists from generalists. Specialists focus on a narrow task; generalists aim to handle multiple tasks and conditions. Today, post-training adapts the broadly trained GR00T model to the SO-101 vial task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","text":"A specialist learns a narrow task. A generalist aims to handle a wider range of tasks; post-training adapts that broad f","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","source_sha256":"875c6cbc0f6733680ad3176522c87e07703808c409f52e5cc06fbbe379a7cc51"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:14","title":"Connecting the robot examples","html":"<p>Apply that idea of task scope to these three examples. A fixed production operation emphasizes repeatability and precision. Manipulating varied objects adds changes in shape, placement, and grasp strategy; handling household jobs broadens the goals and surroundings further. A humanoid body does not establish those capabilities by itself. We judge specialization and generality by the tasks and conditions actually handled.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 2","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","source_sha256":"fa97183478cda613d6528f9c230fa4f462c76ac0effadd599d7b2f46a2fcd61b","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us examine one task closely: how does picking up a vial differ from placing it?</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","kind":"slide","index":4}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"a429a0f7aa7bf4873367e183f434930eb528685e5bf16cb6cd2120a2d746c018","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2","index":4,"title":"Today's Task · Vial Pick-and-Place","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:0","title":"The task and comparison setting","html":"<p>Our task is to pick up scattered vials and place them in a designated rack. The instruction is short, but the requirements change from grasping to transport and then precise placement. To compare those stages meaningfully, we control some surrounding conditions with a lightbox and a limited workspace. Within that setting, we can investigate differences between simulated and physical behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"8271aa74c0ee538a6c923a5a482e25fca174f721e19b484472113d05ab894647","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:5","title":"Motion and success","html":"<p>The motion proceeds through approach, grasp, transport, and placement. Grasping alone is insufficient: the vial must be placed stably in the rack. Recording the failure stage tells us which demonstrations to collect next.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"e72e61d2072cf84a14d84f1ef7d2ecf7cb2a718e74c7d6af5ce79fde66df9088"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:7","title":"Precision from two images","html":"<p>The policy uses two RGB views without depth to determine vial orientation and rack location. After grasping, wrist-view occlusion makes the external view more important. Teleoperating from those same views will make these input limitations concrete.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","text":"Our policy will work from 2D camera information, and the placement of the vials in the rack requires re-orienting the vi","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","source_sha256":"69a772b77b0ea2cd95407c543b972312a638e4969e8a24875a45af44aa532507"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Even a person needs practice with these views. We will now turn those chosen actions into learning examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","kind":"slide","index":5}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"62ee6b24b7e0c742e9e782520e7794e58c38e4527924163ff4763b10a25905ee","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3","index":5,"title":"Learning from Demonstrations and Expanding the Data","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:0","title":"What demonstrations teach","html":"<p>A person can demonstrate when to approach, grasp, and reorient the vial. Imitation learning learns the relationship between those observations and the commands that follow, without requiring us to design a reward for every stage first. The examples therefore matter: successful paths may teach task completion while leaving recovery from unfamiliar states poorly covered.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"8437bca7169c2434f8289f6b4752435472b69ab9c601337b959501f7dfee6098","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:4","title":"From collection to the next experiment","html":"<p>Collect episodes through teleoperation or a scripted controller, then inspect view–action timing, starting conditions, and success or failure segments. New trials after post-training reveal situations missing from the existing data. Use those observations to plan the next collection round.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1 · Collect demonstrations","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"demonstrate"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"32b66798cda5d338a334c5ff4feb958717c587e1b04973e3d047367dd4eb42ef","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:review","title":"2 · Inspect the examples","html":"<p>Inspect the starting pose and camera roles, then follow the episode through grasp and placement. Even a successful task can be a poor training example if images lag or action units disagree. Successful saving and useful data are separate checks.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"review"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"review","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"af0d5b8256746a505a62ce71b29cf0098eaa9d89a0eceaadb6e3e515feecdaa7"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:train","title":"3 · Adapt a pretrained policy","html":"<p>Post-training adjusts the parameters of an observation-to-action function. It is not a procedure for storing a trajectory and replaying it as a file. Useful examples from different arrangements matter; repetition of one scene alone does not establish generalization.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"train"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"train","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"bb44b755f21dd26591f46ca3c467d3053444a7197c39def2c5fe378ce5d576e4"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:evaluate","title":"4 · Test and iterate","html":"<p>Test on placements and starting conditions excluded from training. Splitting neighboring frames from similar episodes does not by itself test unfamiliar situations. Record which conditions were held out so that failures can guide the next collection round.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"evaluate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"evaluate","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"cbbbf8ec64bc09ed5a0b0b3c5f97cac369238f6883c9615bf31589c3b95b5606"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:10","title":"Today’s training stage","html":"<p>Capability can be scaled through pre-training, post-training, and inference. Today we build on broad pre-training and focus on post-training for the SO-101 observation and action interface.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","text":"Broad pre-training, task-specific post-training, and additional computation at inference are different ways to develop c","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","source_sha256":"f584426147c9a01373b8d473fc82332ecf615d9f291754ce23847ae666548ddd"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:14","title":"The cost of robot data","html":"<p>Robot data needs to connect the observed state, the action issued, and the resulting change. Learning requires aligned image and joint timestamps as well as consistent units and robot calibration. Collection cost includes this recording and inspection work in addition to performing the motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-robot-data-takes-work","source_sha256":"f5997e880e8db14a27323051987ff1935415d04bb47070d7a942ab6fcf65f75d"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We know what a useful example contains. How can we produce good examples across varied conditions?</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","kind":"slide","index":6}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"f01f2ba7a48ebbec2fb380538160cdec1fd95d66acfe665fc1be15e64c3320da","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4","index":6,"title":"Why Use Simulation?","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:0","title":"Repeat experiments in simulation","html":"<p>Covering more situations requires repeated changes in object placement. On hardware, someone must reset the objects and check the station. Simulation makes those resets and controlled variations programmable. GPU-based simulation can also support parallel environments, although today’s teleoperation uses one environment. Useful data throughput still depends on rendering, recording, and review, not physics speed alone.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-simulation-matters","source_sha256":"fc77c09fbff63ce4a20ef8c39e7d0c30046d4497ade4af9549261fd5b61a98aa","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:7","title":"Exact state for data generation","html":"<p>Simulation exposes exact object poses, contacts, and segmentation. These can support scripted controllers and episode checks, while the deployed policy uses camera and joint-state inputs available on the physical robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"privileged-information","text":"Simulation also provides access to information that might be impossible to obtain in the real world:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"privileged-information","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"privileged-information","source_sha256":"11101eea2c9839192a9caea334f288f101744546edc6580c206be1af8b4c9d30"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:10","title":"What remains to test on hardware","html":"<p>Simulation expands experimentation but does not eliminate differences in cameras, contact, or actuators. We train with varied simulation data, then use real rollouts to identify the real data or model calibration still needed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"key-takeaways","text":"Simulation enables fast, safe, diverse training that can be impossible in the real world","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"key-takeaways","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"key-takeaways","source_sha256":"7052da3e013f549249c217af18fe2597f61e051b928edcf82216563778d6d2c6"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Repeated experiments need consistent code and guide references. Let us connect the course checkout and verify them.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","kind":"slide","index":7}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-simulation-matters","source_sha256":"c3f3898c2909023496fb8561b3a9c62dd9af8cc1a1cb1b5ab29664b64e4f8645","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1","index":7,"title":"CODEX · Connect the Course Folder and Skills","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:0","title":"Connect the same checkout","html":"<p>We will now connect Codex to the course checkout. The useful property is grounding an answer in the code and guide we are actually running. Select the local aidayseoul2026 checkout that is mounted into the workshop container; in the app, use This computer / Local. Next we will verify the path and the project instructions it reads.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"codex","source_sha256":"ae26390017e07347e9db86245abb69897f14bc1b7eeebf83012567242534661a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:5","title":"Verify the guide and skill","html":"<p>Ask it to read the current path and branch, AGENTS.md, and skills/so101-dli/SKILL.md. Confirm that the reported path matches the course folder so questions refer to the same materials and execution environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-a-chatgpt-app","source_sha256":"b40b384d841831a978ff369c74fa8599426e7b2fa9f889d390d788a55d342dca"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:8","title":"CLI and source references","html":"<p>Run the CLI from the course folder on the host as well. Asking which guide section and code support an answer makes it easier to connect the explanation to the exercise.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-b-codex-cli","source_sha256":"c780240a0e8b2a4a5d8b8dc4b70dc32b959d5334b08f1c7ff938ec83964c21da"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With the connection established, let us make questions specific to the experiment and the evidence available.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","kind":"slide","index":8}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-a-chatgpt-app","source_sha256":"008f93283744d76225fc5e4270bf7fb0ecd584039fed7cdaf58f2089beacd5ec","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2","index":8,"title":"CODEX · Ask Questions and Continue the Exercises","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:0","title":"Include the current state","html":"<p>A useful question includes the goal, the current state, and the evidence. “This command failed while connecting the follower in this container” gives us a better starting point than “the robot does not work.” For conceptual questions, ask for an explanation in terms of today’s vial task so that the answer connects to observations and actions you can inspect.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"environment-setup","source_sha256":"4e363f82010fb5ac7c324a602852f3339aba69a35b6e9b4dd21e65e553d404bf","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:9","title":"Check the document connection","html":"<p>First ask for today’s schedule to verify the document connection. It should match the course schedule in KST: a 09:00 start, lunch from 12:45 to 13:45, and a 17:15 finish.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"first-example-ask-for-todays-schedule","source_sha256":"760e873fcdd48b109327cbc97a958d907300d665b28c29a64d26440273a2319b"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:16","title":"Information for diagnosis","html":"<p>For errors, provide the command, whether it ran on the host or in the container, and the actual error message. For comparisons, include the checkpoint and evaluation conditions. Run motion commands only after preparing the devices and workspace.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","text":"For an error, provide the command, whether it ran on the Host or in the Container, and the relevant error text. Codex ca","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","source_sha256":"ea8a4e6d736e96eade2717a520e88687796c08d9a5ae3bd997fe97131294054d"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>First, distinguish the two arms we are about to connect.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","kind":"slide","index":9}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"environment-setup","source_sha256":"c5ddcd694e428e2834c636366ee2909f99df9e4c87041d60921c37d4bed406ee","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1","index":9,"title":"SO-101 · Leader and Follower","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>First, we will understand the roles of the SO-101 leader and follower and how LeRobot connects data with execution tools. We will also see how to find and use public datasets and community resources.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"04-lerobot.html"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:0","title":"The two arm roles","html":"<p>Here is the first connection from human motion to a robot command. The leader is the input device moved by the operator; the follower performs the task. Teleoperation maps leader joint readings to follower commands. During autonomous evaluation, the policy supplies those commands instead. This lets us check the execution path before asking a learned model to control it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"47fca48e77d74de355cc9a1f77cce737c0c1e0ee22a646f6476925ed02b3ca9b","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:6","title":"Joints and the end effector","html":"<p>Small joint-angle differences propagate through the links into end-effector position errors. Forward kinematics describes that relationship. The effect of a joint error depends on the arm’s pose, so agreement at one pose is not enough; inspect poses relevant to the working range.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show video 1","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Play","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"play"}},{"label":"Pause","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"pause"}},{"label":"Restart video","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"replay"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"ecb0d68edf0695d8cb63ca24bd40661159cbd8027b155afa69e0baacb808a74d","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:10","title":"Mechanical condition matters","html":"<p>SO-101 accessibility and LeRobot support make it suitable for learning the full workflow. Backlash or loose fasteners can change actual motion under the same command, so precise placement failures also require checking the mechanism.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-so-101","text":"What makes it difficult for precision tasks:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"why-so-101","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-so-101","source_sha256":"cda9e219fd85e583fdd3e31a9a793bf00627eb3153140bd82deb6ca8488b5cdc"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Keep that shared follower in mind. LeRobot connects the human and policy paths through its data and execution tools.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","kind":"slide","index":10}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"51c6c0a022e3a2e9dd6526b40fa20d3bdee9057ec2512d20791bbee45ffef70d","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2","index":10,"title":"LeRobot · From Collection to Evaluation","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:0","title":"Why use LeRobot?","html":"<p>LeRobot gives this experiment a common language for data and execution. We can inspect demonstrations from a physical leader or a simulated controller and connect them to training and rollout. A shared file format is only the starting point: the wrist camera key, joint ordering, units, and calibration must carry the same meaning for the data to be compatible.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-lerobot-project","source_sha256":"7d3dd422d4b7e8ba5e3d98b775c4ef97c05cb18c8393d5d9dc69fc7bfc05c7ae","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:9","title":"From local storage to the Hub","html":"<p>Simulation recordings are stored under datasets using the repo_id path. Verify HF_USER and the authenticated account, upload under that namespace, and tag the revision used for training. Inspect local episodes first and check the tag’s revision whenever the data changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"99ab0471542dd0cfab2fd31986ab8c7af0e558fe028877ab93821d32e18e696b"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:13","title":"Train on inspected data","html":"<p>Inspect episodes from lerobot_agent or vials_state_machine in the Visualizer, then post-train GR00T with lerobot-train. Keep the input dataset ID distinct from the model repository and output directory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","text":"Collect demonstrations with lerobot_agent and the leader arm, or use vials_state_machine for automated simulation collec","accordion":{"key":"lerobot-workflow","item":"post-training-pipeline"}}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"ddedde30289b3131c408fa2bc1f3c5aa5535fdb59acae748aa68fbcdd5ee0817"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:16","title":"Run the learned policy","html":"<p>In simulation, the policy server performs inference and lerobot_eval runs the environment. On hardware, lerobot-rollout uses the calibrated follower and camera configuration. Both require matching the checkpoint and its expected inputs.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","text":"In the same container terminal, start python -m lerobot.async_inference.policy_server in the background, then run lerobo","accordion":{"key":"lerobot-workflow","item":"simulation-evaluation"}}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"fc208886f1c237c4b5c27e576ed387b40d9748a860670944a553adb298b29711"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:20","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The dataset is an intermediate artifact in this workflow. Let us open a Hub episode and examine its suitability for learning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","kind":"slide","index":11}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-lerobot-project","source_sha256":"217a60d2f2da9cd952a2cad45423c2757eb6d54eeae9edbbb7d759ba919ebb16","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3","index":11,"title":"Hugging Face Hub and Dataset Visualizer","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:0","title":"Open a public Hub dataset","html":"<p>Let me demonstrate how to inspect public data shared through LeRobot and the Hub. Open FineART, or enter lerobot/FineART and select GO. It uses a different robot from SO-101, so first check its camera configuration and joint dimensions.</p><p><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/lerobot/visualize_dataset?path=/lerobot/FineART/episode_0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Dataset Visualizer ↗</a></p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","source_sha256":"5ea6bca8558e6d7a5fb5d6f8f35c2102862a7ea359526b608aa111c7da72050b"},"instructor_action":{"kind":"web-demo","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/lerobot/visualize_dataset?path=/lerobot/FineART/episode_0"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:3","title":"Read images and trajectories together","html":"<p>Read the camera views together with state and action at the same point in time. State is the measured pose; action is the commanded target, so they need not match during motion. Check whether gripper closure follows the command change and whether the recording continues through placement. We are checking the relationship between training inputs and targets, rather than simply watching a successful video.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","text":"Use this tool to explore any LeRobot dataset on the Hub. You can scrub through episodes, view camera feeds, and inspect ","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"dataset-visualizer","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","source_sha256":"f35cbb85a45cb4a383ba34ef625bc86c8564a976e61b70513dee045f1dd11493","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:15","title":"Sharing and reproducibility","html":"<p>When training on multiple datasets, check both schema compatibility and how often each source is sampled. Record the dataset revisions, training settings, checkpoint, and evaluation conditions together to reproduce the experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","text":"For co-training we combined multiple data sources into a single training dataset or ran multiple passesof training.","accordion":{"key":"hugging-face-use-cases","item":"hf-co-training-merge"}}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","source_sha256":"ebddda9cebdc6c1d187113b02cf6b9d4052c300250ae4016b775600ec063ce9d"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:21","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Input consistency begins in the physical workspace. Let us establish the scene and devices we will record.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","kind":"slide","index":12}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"community-resources","source_sha256":"1a29696b3286e3f4a2b81b54234cc79ceb13e3e33ee49fbbae94ea727d346b0d","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","index":12,"title":"Workspace · Check Lighting, Props, and Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:0","title":"A workspace that produces observations","html":"<p>Data quality starts here, before a file is saved. A moving camera mount or changing light can make the same object produce different observations. Matching the reference workspace is part of controlling the inputs to training and evaluation. We will check the lighting and layout first, then verify the result in both actual camera views.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"using-the-workspace","source_sha256":"cfc1d2f536691db58d6977fdc52ae1b03055f06d532016015307a5636fda06f3","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:5","title":"Lighting and layout","html":"<p>Unlock and power the light continuously. Choose brightness within the guide’s 50–100% range and keep it fixed during collection and evaluation. Facing the lightbox, place the rack on the left and the pickup area on the right, with one to three vials and empty rack slots.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"turn-on-the-light","text":"Press the light's power button.","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"turn-on-the-light","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"turn-on-the-light","source_sha256":"180381cb5066511d9f9f2cd6d1905dab1c3e63c4ed0218ef31b7a909681aba62"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:10","title":"Mounting and visibility","html":"<p>Check clamps and cables, and ensure the external view includes both rack and pickup area. The wrist view should be clear at close range. Stop the robot before reaching in to reposition objects.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"check-before-moving","text":"Make sure the robot clamp is secure and cables are clear of the arm’s motion.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"check-before-moving","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"check-before-moving","source_sha256":"13017e6400cb105c21a669fdf214ec8a41c75b0f76ddf7f60f7a530f408796cb"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With the scene ready, check that commands reach the intended arm, starting with power and port identification.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","kind":"slide","index":13}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"using-the-workspace","source_sha256":"f07c916f1d2b723afba3b0ba11627ec8bfd146b484b68da4c89896430c7a5fb2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","index":13,"title":"Calibration Preparation · Power and Ports","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>This unit focuses on safely checking power and ports for both arms and connecting encoder readings to physical poses through calibration. We will start by identifying the devices.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:0","title":"Power and device identity","html":"<p>We will identify the devices step by step. The leader uses 5 V and the follower uses 12 V; their power connections must not be interchanged. A working USB connection also does not establish that motor power is correct. After checking power and LEDs, we will use the port finder to associate each USB device with its arm.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"safety-guidelines","source_sha256":"0adbe990644695d9e20423db317067c4f16f087766c37e99d36425d51fbc3264","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:14","title":"Where commands run","html":"<p>Start the workshop container from the actual repository folder on the host, or attach with docker exec if it is already running. That folder supplies the source, datasets, and outputs mounts. Run subsequent device commands inside the container.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"run-the-docker-container-for-this-course","source_sha256":"6445e56d2dec69e92b1694ab9c42d13a54d8fd22fd4f9efe30754c1c81711a48"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:19","title":"Map ports to roles","html":"<p>Follow the port finder to unplug the leader USB, identify the disappearing port, and reconnect it. Repeat for the follower. Use the actual results for each device rather than copying example ttyACM numbers.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-teleop-arm-port","source_sha256":"fdd64e2820893faa25cbaf79cd0588d5e214d86daee46c557be0e35357aaa41c"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:23","title":"Save ports and IDs","html":"<p>Store the leader port and ID in the TELEOP variables and the follower values in the ROBOT variables. Ports select devices; IDs distinguish calibration files. setenv persists values in docker/env, but recheck ports after reconnecting USB.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":3,"blocks":"3"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-teleop-arm-port","source_sha256":"5cba4e974f54810ffb33933d39cca17ae44edf487fb66ae90c98a24907a4b98d"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:32","title":"Ready for calibration","html":"<p>Check that the printed ports and IDs match the physical arms. Continue with leader and follower calibration in the same container environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":4,"kind":"command","index":2,"blocks":"2"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-robot-arm-port","source_sha256":"21dbf964d55ad6d575247d81974c77aee2058fc869a773179bf9daf391d55a09"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:35","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have identified the devices. Calibration now connects their numeric readings to physical poses.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","kind":"slide","index":14}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"safety-guidelines","source_sha256":"c9841e8c19d2f11af8e5791424b8015e0992d6e8e8040bcef76bafca3ff16cd7","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","index":14,"title":"Leader and Follower Calibration","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:0","title":"What calibration aligns","html":"<p>An encoder gives us a number; the policy needs a consistent interpretation of robot pose. Calibration relates that number and its range to the physical arm. If the reference is wrong, the same joint value can produce a different end-effector position from the one represented in training. We will calibrate each arm and check both the saved files and live readings.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibration-process","source_sha256":"e6fbc98b6cdd9b4dde3910c6dfd8656010fda80eb81f1bbb311902ea57d7550a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:5","title":"Reference pose and range sweep","html":"<p>Run leader calibration and press Enter at the pictured midpoint pose. Set wrist roll accurately in the initial pose, but exclude it from the range sweep. Move the other joints through their full ranges individually, without cable or object interference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-leader-arm","source_sha256":"8cd2d3544ef120d40f0731fc955ce4d9663f04514db4a5b977a45cfd28177320"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:9","title":"Read the checker result","html":"<p>The checker compares saved ranges with the mean and ±2σ range of ten references per arm type. WARN flags differences to investigate. A static PASS does not verify current encoders; inspect live OUT OF RANGE results separately on the connected arm.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-leader-arm","source_sha256":"cf84ec7c469bab748cad5f0035a2a3139f99dad3894ed62e3a13ab09df801aea"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:16","title":"Repeat for the follower","html":"<p>Repeat with ROBOT_PORT and ROBOT_ID for the follower. Correct initial wrist-roll orientation avoids reading wraparound. Verify the follower file and live checks so teleoperation interprets joint values consistently.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-follower-arm","source_sha256":"7a723541a3f9bb04dd64194d66b827137c7c0f68d4bb2bac7d5027f21f8383fb"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:26","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Next, camera observations must retain the meaning they had during training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","kind":"slide","index":15}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibration-process","source_sha256":"4c648b9aeadc3fe876bfae0843490af13993d830d7f7a896bb7c426d26ddcbb6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","index":15,"title":"Camera and Calibration · Final Checks","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:0","title":"Align the camera","html":"<p>With the joint references established, we need to align what the robot sees. Changing camera angle or distance changes the apparent position and scale of the rack. Start with the external camera tilted about 54 degrees down from horizontal to match the supplied policy’s reference view. The final check is the actual framing of the rack, vials, and robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"173a6408f81782701ca26dba79343413659e687922ddec75c8dbd4929c66c5b4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:3","title":"From angle to framing","html":"<p>The angle is a starting point for framing. Compare the robot, rack, and vial positions and field of view with simulation in the live image. Proceed once both the arm’s port, ID, and calibration and the camera view are aligned.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","text":"Place the long edge of your phone on the table and check that it reads approximately 0°.","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"d1e18d8f60760be73d7511c480dc02bea74b74865e01c26f63c8ea4c94c2e995"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:8","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With arm and camera references established, verify physical motion using small human commands.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","kind":"slide","index":16}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"b654d78edeb074cc684d9fd07fd9b287bc147a4ac293b8849dd8eec0b9e1b578","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","index":16,"title":"Real Teleoperation · Safety and Basic Motion","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to teleoperate the follower with the leader and verify the camera views the policy will receive. We will establish safe starting and stopping procedures, then inspect images and motion together in Rerun.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"08-operating-so101.html"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:0","title":"Prepare the first motion","html":"<p>For the first motion, we will check the leader–follower connection without a learned policy. Bring the starting poses close together and clear the path the follower may take to align. Begin with small movements and confirm that the intended joints respond. Resolving errors here will help us distinguish a policy prediction problem from a device problem later.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperating","source_sha256":"2ee36de173b7eaf49c0293166d3b2c15daea6d6c454e70d77de0d0a641d1eb5c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:8","title":"Safety and stopping","html":"<p>Keep hands away from powered follower joints and the gripper. Check finger clearance at the leader joints and cable routing. Stop unexpected motion with Ctrl+C and keep power disconnection accessible if needed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"safety","text":"Before powering on or running policies:","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"safety","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"safety","source_sha256":"2b4820ed40ced4094c1910841e151860cbc293fdb52158a85982011b6887f177"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:12","title":"Basic teleoperation","html":"<p>Check types, ports, and IDs and run basic teleoperation without cameras. Move the leader slowly, verify follower response, and then try moving a vial. Stop before adding camera views for the next run.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperation-no-cameras","source_sha256":"48b8161ad8a3874a27f37249b322c29961f929f493e2d89c3b648fc62e8829d2"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Remember how direct operation felt. Compare it with the information available through the two cameras alone.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","kind":"slide","index":17}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"safety","source_sha256":"3a1c3c4fb9df5f76ada10c3dbb6888f167a277c3a9a9b5320c3c78a4ba2eb02a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","index":17,"title":"Teleoperation with Two Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:0","title":"Use the policy’s views","html":"<p>Now operate through the two camera views rather than looking directly at the workspace. Distance and occlusion can become ambiguous in images even when they seem obvious in person. The wrist view shows local grasp detail; the external view shows the rack and the wider motion. Notice when each view becomes useful—we will use that experience to interpret policy failures.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"camera-setup","source_sha256":"92c30b81e466296255428b9b8b96c36418e32d7d584339ab301fbffbc00b90f4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:9","title":"Identify camera indices","html":"<p>Open the camera finder’s previews in outputs/captured_images on the host and map each index to its actual view. Assign distinct devices to wrist and external and recheck indices after reconnecting USB.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"finding-available-cameras","source_sha256":"5cd5586af7a787378464f4c82fa7c19694632553a36fd36dd928f2ae51410819"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:22","title":"Operate and inspect through images","html":"<p>Run teleoperation with the verified camera settings and display_data. Check the assignments in Rerun, then attempt grasp and placement using only the images. Note where the target becomes occluded and stop with Ctrl+C when finished.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperation-with-cameras","source_sha256":"78bc5bb5ad3ce99643ea47fce66a05d0d906d82537a228bae7cabc40020c3f31"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:31","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>You have experienced choosing motion from images and pose. Now connect that process to what GR00T learns.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","kind":"slide","index":18}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"camera-setup","source_sha256":"701ddf6fe0581abe2548afaa34f7c0458c8da24c384cbc9873bf485cd076e756","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1","index":18,"title":"Isaac GR00T · Vision–Language–Action","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will understand how GR00T turns images, language, and robot state into actions and how this differs from a conventional robot pipeline. Then we will compare batch size, steps, and horizons and distinguish optimizer state, schedules, and data sampling when designing continuation experiments.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--10-groot--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"10-groot.html"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:0","title":"VLA inputs and outputs","html":"<p>A moment ago, you interpreted two images and chose the next movement. GR00T receives images, a task instruction, and the current joint state for that decision. Vision describes the scene, language specifies the goal, and proprioception supplies the robot’s own pose. A Vision–Language–Action model uses these inputs to generate the next sequence of actions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-is-gr00t","source_sha256":"95231fe80cd6648db26c07ce560c81c2dcf3383fa0f8fbcc39ca2172e1b93ad6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:12","title":"Learning the action mapping","html":"<p>A modular pipeline explicitly designs intermediate perception, planning, and control representations. A VLA learns an observation-to-action relationship from data to handle task and scene variation. Motor control, calibration, and preprocessing still remain. Knowing the boundary between the learned policy and explicit control layers tells us where to investigate a failure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","text":"A modular robot pipeline might estimate an object’s pose, plan a motion, and send that plan to a controller. A VLA learn","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","source_sha256":"335a18b01a69e45c50a5b367eacd3959953b15f997947ccc5f7e91ac059e3c96","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:14","title":"The data pyramid and our data","html":"<p>Human video and web data provide broad visual and language knowledge, while simulation and robot demonstrations connect it to action. We add SO-101 experience to this pretrained foundation for the vial task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-data-pyramid","text":"Human Videos & Web Data: Learn human behavior and visual-language knowledge.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"the-data-pyramid","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"the-data-pyramid","source_sha256":"b3d627352302f3fd92eb2b4c3a18f9cea10228640b92d09024e1e5d22ce65ce4"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:17","title":"Training versus inference","html":"<p>Training changes model weights using a loss; hyperparameters such as batch size and learning rate govern that process. Post-training adapts pretrained weights to the task. Inference keeps those weights fixed and computes actions for new observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"defining-terminology","text":"Model weights, or parameters, are the numerical values learned inside a neural network. We use the two terms interchange","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"defining-terminology","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"defining-terminology","source_sha256":"5836a0a7304127044d97bcdbc333186e75819aadb342532819c6ffc1a4db8a98"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:24","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With inputs and outputs defined, examine the components connecting them and what changes during training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","kind":"slide","index":19}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-is-gr00t","source_sha256":"b52ba3b2017da9599752a7b49a794736b363b267773c2c6d4402ed0f4f282bef","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2","index":19,"title":"GR00T · Training, Inference, Architecture","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:0","title":"Which weights learn?","html":"<p>After choosing pretrained weights, we still have to decide which parameters our demonstrations will update. The baseline recipe freezes vision and language while training the action components. Frozen components still compute features; the optimizer simply does not update their parameters. Adapting visual features as well is another experiment, with different data requirements, memory use, and overfitting risks.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"training-and-inference","source_sha256":"fc63a360fcced69a8c365be8cb90ac6f6a3f78b510f7aaace1f5a66bd68bd52d","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:foundation","title":"Begin with pretrained model weights","html":"<p>Pretraining provides reusable representations and a foundation for behavior. It does not automatically align every joint convention or vial-grasp strategy with our SO-101. Task-specific demonstrations make that correspondence concrete in the next stage.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"foundation"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"foundation","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"2d718f8d201f28cf095b9fd616c8f26925eaf6f06c6de8baca99bdf01eb48277"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:demonstrations","title":"Sample examples of this robot and task","html":"<p>Sample an observation and the action chunk that follows it from an episode. Images, instruction, and joint state must refer to the same task and moment. Repeated sampling can overrepresent particular layouts or long pauses if those dominate the eligible windows.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"demonstrations"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"demonstrations","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"39637c02619c165abeb5184d9fa9eb86285ecbbc837922db91aadefd6e53cbaf"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:components","title":"Choose which weights can change","html":"<p>Frozen components still compute features, but the optimizer does not update their parameters. The base workshop example keeps vision and language frozen while training the action components.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"components"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"components"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:update","title":"Use loss to guide weight updates","html":"<p>The flow-matching objective produces a loss and gradients, and the optimizer updates the selected weights. That loss measures agreement with the training objective, not rack-placement success itself. Training logs and rollouts answer complementary questions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"update"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"update","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"4728de3f02d613988f4ac318adc7c144150d7db3a6feb3767386cf59e4ac7df2"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:unfreeze","title":"Optionally adapt visual features","html":"<p>Unfreezing vision can adapt features to our camera appearance, but limited data can also encourage background overfitting or alter useful existing representations. If several recipe settings change together, their result cannot isolate the effect of vision tuning. Record all changed factors in the comparison.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"unfreeze"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"unfreeze","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"8fbf7de2d62a7f301a0d33f26be80709c31092f5a9b72506279237f92977ac1a"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:inference","title":"Use the adapted policy with fixed weights","html":"<p>Inference takes fresh images, instruction text, and robot state and predicts actions using the saved checkpoint and processors. Feedback changes the next observation, not the checkpoint weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"inference"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:12","title":"From backbone to action head","html":"<p>Now place those components in the information flow. GR00T 1.7 uses a Cosmos-Reason2-2B backbone to provide image-and-language features. The action head conditions on those features and robot state to produce an action chunk. Flow matching learns a transformation from noise toward demonstrated action distributions. This backbone has a different output and role from the Cosmos video augmentation we will examine later.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"architecture-overview","text":"GR00T 1.7 conditions action generation on images, language, and robot state. Its vision-language backbone is Cosmos-Reas","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"architecture-overview","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"architecture-overview","source_sha256":"c20c4f6b488ecb73d0ad90f6e1a0030ec64b34880c08ac9380f2bf83fd4b5b28","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:vision","title":"Vision: observe the task","html":"<p>The wrist view moves with the hand while the external view provides a wider reference. Complementary views only help when their roles remain consistent with training. Swapping their keys or geometry changes the meaning of the inputs, which is why we inspect both names and actual images.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"vision"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"vision","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"38f365c533a6aa1edfd339a395dd7a03418c44489d6349583f6ca43b5e5bbf36"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:language","title":"Language: specify the task","html":"<p>Language identifies the requested task. “Place the vial in the rack” does not specify every joint target. The model uses its learned relationship between that instruction, the scene, state, and demonstrations, so the instruction and the recorded behavior must describe the same task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"language"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"language","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"f9973dc4f51b60a8caae69c628577c04f06be45a6ea625ce28440d78b369b14f"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:reason","title":"System 2: interpret images and language","html":"<p>Cosmos-Reason2-2B supplies the vision-language features used by the action head. “System 2” is a conceptual label for this perception and reasoning role, not a separate planner you program in this workshop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"reason"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"reason"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:action","title":"System 1: generate a chunk of actions","html":"<p>The action head uses backbone features and robot state to generate a sequence of future actions. Predicting them jointly can represent temporally coordinated motion. The environment may change during execution, so deciding how many actions to execute before observing again is a separate control choice.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"action"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"action","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"220751d649c0f2655a649900c12a6df1b298b5b8e77e8c4f487eda419cf0f487"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:control","title":"Act, then observe the result","html":"<p>The rollout controller applies returned actions and obtains fresh observations for later predictions. Feedback changes the model’s inputs; ordinary inference does not update its weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"control"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"control"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:20","title":"Processors and control","html":"<p>Joint-state inputs require consistent ordering, units, and calibration. The processor converts model actions into executable commands, distinguishing relative arm targets from the absolute gripper target. New observations after execution inform the next chunk.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Robot state: where the arm is now","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"state"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"architecture-overview","source_sha256":"373f54594a9ed69130391dff887f699561fc83b617346407db4175f5c19f908f"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:27","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>How does this structure participate in one weight update? Examine batch size and action windows with concrete numbers.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","kind":"slide","index":20}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"training-and-inference","source_sha256":"bba6d000d421c5ace20d3d1901883a041ba95d710168fe0ba4d71bfddc9b86b2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3","index":20,"title":"One GR00T Training Step","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:0","title":"Inputs to one update","html":"<p>Let us zoom in on one optimizer update. We sample observations and their following action windows, compute a batch loss and gradients, and update the trainable weights. A window is not an entire episode. Without gradient accumulation, effective batch is per-process batch times the number of processes; accumulation adds another factor. We will make these distinctions concrete with the diagram.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"35134a33af555a316bf6d196154547b3e90c156db7a823615e4c4fcfd261af69","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:7654a288eb","title":"Baseline: one update","html":"<p>Start with batch 32 per GPU, one GPU process, and horizon 16. One update covers 32 windows and 512 target timesteps. These are sampled training targets, including repeats, rather than distinct episodes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Baseline: one update","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:d26c8c75ea","title":"What if batch doubles?","html":"<p>Keep GPU count and horizon fixed and change only batch to 64. The result is 64 windows and 1,024 target timesteps. Each update sees twice as many examples, but the update count is unchanged. Memory and runtime need separate measurement; this does not imply twice the success rate.</p>","actions":[{"label":"What if batch doubles?","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":64,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:74a625bd56","title":"Same target count, different temporal coverage","html":"<p>Now restore batch 32 and increase horizon to 32. The total is still 1,024 targets, but each of the 32 windows covers about 1.07 seconds of commands. Batch changes the number of examples; horizon changes temporal coverage per example. Equal products do not make the learning problem identical.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Same target count, different temporal coverage","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":32},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:1355f3326b","title":"Return to the baseline horizon","html":"<p>Restore horizon 16. At 30 Hz, nominal execution coverage is about 0.53 seconds; the first-to-last target timestamp span is 0.50 seconds. Next, see how these targets produce a loss and update the weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Return to the baseline horizon","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:batch","title":"Batch size","html":"<p>Batch size counts sampled observation/action windows, not whole episodes. Each window contains camera images, the instruction, joint state, and an action target chunk starting at the sampled frame.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"batch"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"batch"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:targets","title":"Training horizon","html":"<p>The training horizon counts action targets paired with one observation, starting at the sampled frame with indices 0 through H−1. At 30 Hz, 16 commands cover about 0.53 seconds of nominal control; the first-to-last target timestamp span is 0.50 seconds. Distinguish sequence coverage from the interval between its endpoint timestamps.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"targets"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"targets","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"423c9298cb5804685371737ecd49a1c335d79d7a19a8a4006d1e3ed5bea8ec3b"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:7","title":"From loss to weight update","html":"<p>Flow matching mixes target actions and noise at a sampled flow time and computes masked MSE between predicted and target flows. Backpropagation and configured gradient clipping precede the optimizer update. Learning rate and schedule govern update size; checkpoint rollouts assess task performance.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Trainable components","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"loss"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"1466741716fe54c00cc928d52dbc38e51e5769af6317b3d33aff989a2e66a254"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:update","title":"Learning rate","html":"<p>After backpropagation, the loop clips the gradient norm when configured, then the optimizer updates trainable weights. Learning rate scales this update together with the optimizer.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"update"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"update"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:3","title":"Interpret training time","html":"<p>The guide records about 12.5 and 13.5 hours for two 20,000-step runs on one H100. These recipe-specific measurements include checkpoint saving and exclude data preparation and evaluation. Our hardware requires its own timing measurement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","text":"Our verified sim-only and mixed HG-DAgger 20,000-step runs took about 12½ and 13½ hours , respectively, on a single H100","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"155247326bac713e3b2e4ed0773657c52f1a64b3bc2fe7a1474201c14768da11"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We understand one update. Which settings and artifacts are needed to reproduce the experiment?</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","kind":"slide","index":21}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"a9f4e0a9b6cf5e4cc0f89ab4a54c96332baea7150a153a27e9dcd0ee10627b7f","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4","index":21,"title":"Post-Training · Configuration and Validation","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:0","title":"The post-training setup","html":"<p>A training recipe is an experiment record, not just a command. Dataset revision, base model, trainable components, and training budget give a checkpoint its meaning. In class we evaluate prepared checkpoints. For an independent run, keep the reviewed input dataset, model repository, and output directory distinct, and retain the resolved configuration so that the comparison can be reproduced.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"fa1aed5b0324fb8200740eb50ac2dbf988b3f5c50334c06881ede10dd2340e38","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:6","title":"Reference recipe and comparisons","html":"<p>The reference recipe uses 20,000 updates, checkpoints every 5,000 steps, BF16, and image augmentation. Batch is per GPU process, so changing batch or GPU count changes data exposure at fixed steps. The vision-unfrozen alternative also changes settings such as chunk length; its difference is not a vision-only effect.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"cb03cea89a50eab8d594f24c6a28e76d8585c54214c53b9acc20e969c9770f84"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:17","title":"Resume versus fresh fine-tuning","html":"<p>Resume restores weights, optimizer, scheduler, and the update counter. Starting a new run from checkpoint weights creates a fresh optimizer and schedule and counts from zero. Thus, 20,000 fresh updates from 50,000-step weights and resuming toward a total of 70,000 have different starting states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Choose What to Restore","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"1c9612b24658fb97ebad8b14cca4332f901683365ebaa8b1c57cfb64069dcbd7"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:22","title":"Checkpoint compatibility","html":"<p>Load checkpoint weights together with configuration and processors. Camera keys, joint representation, calibration, and base revision must agree. The same gripper percentage can represent different openings under different calibration; any required mapping must cover observations before normalization and actions after decoding.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"A gripper percentage can mean a different physical opening when its encoder calibration differs. Collection and simulati","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"a23d5f5f7b4b6b92b8fd389fb6edd042090938b71010f4629be7cb1a7963d006"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:27","title":"Mixture and actual sampling","html":"<p>Stored episode proportions can differ from training sampling proportions. The workshop path shuffles eligible frames after merging, so longer episodes contribute more examples. Enforcing a particular sim/real mixture requires a source-aware sampler.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Two useful experiments are starting from the simulation-trained checkpoint and continuing on mixed sim/real data, or sta","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"6f9f921b2dfbc2808f3650791010d5f6e3ecc3985b8a14e86c847f57b5d753e4"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:30","title":"Measure improvement","html":"<p>Held-out validation measures predictions on unseen demonstrations; rollout measures task completion. Keep test data out of normalization statistics as well. If loss falls while unfamiliar positions fail, inspect starting poses and coverage before adding updates, and compare intermediate checkpoints under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Keep some demonstrations aside for testing: never use them to train the model or calculate the normalization statistics ","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"9563f8135cc2c58b5e751ece4bcd015077a6f057af0c06d68a63971f32878cbd"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:video:2:0","title":"Look beyond the loss","html":"<p>In this example, the policy waits until the vial is repositioned near the gripper. Evaluation with changed object positions helps reveal whether it relies on familiar states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":2,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:42","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Loading a checkpoint is not enough: its actions must be interpreted correctly. Distinguish their meaning from their execution length.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","kind":"slide","index":22}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"82904aea150a97ad5bde76c6b35f1d18be097e86132fedd008f7f2b41a0b114e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5","index":22,"title":"Action Space · Prediction and Execution","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:0","title":"What an action represents","html":"<p>What does an action actually command? Joint actions specify joint targets, while end-effector actions specify a hand pose that needs a conversion such as inverse kinematics. Here we use joint-position actions: relative targets for the arm and an absolute target for the gripper. Relative values only have meaning with the correct reference state and decoding procedure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-representations","source_sha256":"7841cf377cfbae363644b920e90ebab6cb49cc36d533c39c3b18b2ba52ccc89e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:10","title":"Prediction and execution lengths","html":"<p>chunk_size defines the training target window, n_action_steps affects the returned sequence, and the client’s action_horizon or engine horizon affects execution. Predicting a longer sequence does not require executing it all. Our comparison uses a 16-action rollout; choosing the RTC engine with enabled=false leaves compensation disabled. Read the next calculations as nominal intervals excluding latency.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-representations","source_sha256":"981f3034fb69f5e96cff83045e20a704dfe444ff051d58fcb5ce75df0f1bdf12","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:d96efa7a30","title":"Training baseline","html":"<p>Before comparing execution lengths, check training exposure. Batch 32, one GPU, and 20,000 updates give 640,000 sampled windows. Repeated samples count again; this is not 640,000 new examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Training baseline","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:6cb131690b","title":"Change batch, keep updates","html":"<p>Changing batch to 64 gives 1,280,000 windows over the same 20,000 updates. Equal update counts can mean different data exposure. This is why comparisons must record batch and GPU count as well.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Change batch, keep updates","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":64,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:0f0dcea6c4","title":"Match total exposure","html":"<p>Reduce updates to 10,000 at batch 64 and total exposure returns to 640,000 windows. Exposure matches the baseline, but there are half as many weight updates. Schedules and gradient composition differ, so performance and runtime need not match. Now switch to inference with fixed weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Match total exposure","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":64,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":10000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:74b3176945","title":"Execution horizon 16","html":"<p>With the baseline execution horizon of 16 at 30 Hz, a new prediction is used about every 533 ms, or 1.88 times per second. The robot still receives 30 commands per second. These nominal intervals exclude inference and network delay.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Execution horizon 16","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000,"#groot-horizon":16},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:b8e8b175aa","title":"Reconsider every 8 actions","html":"<p>Reduce only the execution horizon to 8: about 267 ms between predictions, or 3.75 predictions per second. There is an earlier opportunity to use a new observation, at twice the inference request rate. The robot command rate does not become 60 Hz.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Reconsider every 8 actions","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-horizon":8},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:e8b45e3b7f","title":"Compare 4 actions and interpret","html":"<p>Horizon 4 gives about 133 ms and 7.50 predictions per second—four times the baseline request rate. Better responsiveness also requires new predictions to be ready in time. Shorter is not always better: compare actual latency and task success together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Compare 4 actions and interpret","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-horizon":4},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:8b775b995a","title":"Restore the baseline for the exercise","html":"<p>Restore horizon 16. These controls change only the teaching illustration, not the training configuration or robot controller. Next, record the observations and actions in simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Restore the baseline for the exercise","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000,"#groot-horizon":16},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:19","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We know which inputs and targets to record. Create those pairs with a real leader and simulated follower.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","kind":"slide","index":23}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-space-and-control","source_sha256":"cf0a0cfc766e0608c25ee525d111ebfe791526c50f29c60dd4b2155fc3d5bddd","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","index":23,"title":"Sim Teleoperation · Launch and Camera Views","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to move the simulated SO-101 with the physical leader, use both camera views, and record and review demonstrations. After learning the teleoperation workflow, we will look at optional Hub sharing and scripted collection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:0","title":"Physical leader, simulated follower","html":"<p>We will now create those observation–action pairs ourselves. Keep the physical leader and replace the follower with the simulated SO-101. Stop real teleoperation first, then use the calibrated leader’s port and ID. Before recording, check the joint mapping and both camera views at this boundary between the physical input device and the simulated robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"teleoperation-collecting-human-demonstrations","source_sha256":"00581155a798a8620837a3df096eb9b16dee30513a274bc2f42b930dd20ac58c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:17","title":"Start the task and cameras","html":"<p>Specify the DR task, cameras all, and both newton and ovrtx_renderer presets. The task ID selects the scene and randomization; camera and renderer settings provide image inputs. Check motion and both views before enabling recording.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"practice-teleoperation-in-simulation","source_sha256":"feba599bdf1f2dd112be38b02c5c831bfd20e3b0bccbec614ee67026147103e3"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:21","title":"Practice in the browser","html":"<p>Connect one browser tab to localhost:8090 on the host. With that tab focused, use R to reset the layout and perform the task from the camera views. These keyboard controls belong to the browser path and do not carry over to Rerun or headless execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","text":"Open http://localhost:8090 in the host browser. Connect only one browser tab to the viewer at a time.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","source_sha256":"c786bbeef2d85ba1f5b900e1a5a8f4043d5f1381fd02fbd58f8879a9b852a7aa"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:23","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Visible motion and saved training data are separate outcomes. Next, record a complete episode and inspect it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","kind":"slide","index":24}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"teleoperation-collecting-human-demonstrations","source_sha256":"78eb7346775d77636d5434c01445edaf5473f7fecb3b86575347e37de5b68c7a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","index":24,"title":"Demonstrations · Record, Review, and Expand","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:0","title":"The recorded time relationship","html":"<p>The timing of a training example matters. The current images and joint state form the observation; the target applied next is the action. Measured state and commanded target need not be equal. A one-frame shift can associate an action with the wrong observation. Timestamps, the instruction, and episode boundaries help preserve the sequence the learner is supposed to model.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"inside-a-demonstration","source_sha256":"07d7049ddbf0b11af119f7a4b0bc42eaa6ce568a8742b7c5d3d3706d274c3a9e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:images","title":"What the robot saw","html":"<p>An observation contains the wrist and external camera views. Each camera’s name, mount pose, field of view, and frame timing should match between recording and policy use.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"images"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"images"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:state","title":"Where the robot was","html":"<p>Joint state records the robot’s measured joint positions. Joint state may differ from the commanded state because motion takes time and real actuators do not track commands perfectly.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"state"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"state"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:action","title":"What was commanded","html":"<p>An action is the joint command sent after the observation is captured: from the leader arm during recording, and from the policy during evaluation. Its joint order, units and calibration must agree with the robot interface and the training setup.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"action"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"action"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:episode","title":"Keep the sequence together","html":"<p>Timestamps and episode boundaries preserve the sequence of the task. The instruction describes the task performed in that sequence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"episode"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"episode"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:17","title":"Choose storage and record","html":"<p>Set HF_USER and repo_id and add the task instruction to start recording into datasets/&lt;repo_id&gt;. Use resume to append or a new repo_id for a separate experiment. Local collection is separate from Hub authentication and upload.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"record-demonstrations","source_sha256":"ad631deffd58dfd3093803427018289d44e3550b69bad56e401878056e726309"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:21","title":"Saving versus resetting","html":"<p>With browser focus, Space starts a take and Space again saves it. C discards it; R discards and resets the environment. Saving runs in the background, so wait for completion and allow shutdown and encoding to finish at session end.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"record-demonstrations","text":"Open http://localhost:8090. Connect only one browser tab to the viewer at a time.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"record-demonstrations","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"record-demonstrations","source_sha256":"7afdd0eddb652d433733e694d7e63a14ca29a1b9c26d386179e0e4cd6ea7813a"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:26","title":"Inspect before sharing","html":"<p>Open the repo ID, local root, and episode index in the Visualizer to inspect timing, grasp, and placement. Upload the inspected local dataset under the matching Hub namespace. After updates, ensure the v3.0 tag points to the intended revision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"review-and-upload-collected-data","source_sha256":"1e3f9ea69e5452ec618b87f9819dc7b8c9e798a22b73511e429afabe0b030f25"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:39","title":"Scale with scripted collection","html":"<p>vials_state_machine replaces the leader with a scripted controller. Inspect a preview before recording. A target of five means five successful episodes; failed attempts are excluded. Collection does not use Space/C, and the dataset is exported after reaching the target, so allow export to finish.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"optional-automated-data-collection","source_sha256":"87768f01ca352d43769aa2f3c3f7e401f85a0a2cf90ceef5be9e88319a6fbb5b"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:45","title":"Choose the collection method","html":"<p>Simulation supports repeated resets and layouts; real data captures actual cameras, contact, and actuator responses. Scripted and human collection also provide different motions. Add data that fills missing positions, views, and behaviors rather than merely increasing counts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"optional-automated-data-collection","source_sha256":"a656a3b912b221accfb65852aacfb504975f881f6432ea0a72be70768f8e152d"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:52","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We can inspect an episode. Next, broaden the conditions represented, rather than only increasing the example count.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","kind":"slide","index":25}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"recording-demonstrations","source_sha256":"c9b748c792806b0aad055297eb167d98878269a817c6936e8d85d5346e2e8bb0","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","index":25,"title":"Domain Randomization · Learning to Handle Variation","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will explain which differences domain randomization addresses and how changed conditions become part of the demonstrations. We will distinguish what to vary from the conditions used for evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"10-domain-randomization.html"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:0","title":"The mismatch DR addresses","html":"<p>Repeating a demonstration from one layout does not create experience of new conditions. Domain Randomization defines ranges for variables such as lighting or placement and collects examples under those conditions. The goal is to reduce dependence on incidental scene details. The variation still needs to be relevant to deployment and compatible with completing the task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"011303a41776272d449c6f5cfd0df921ece8d7f6e95f58c3c36f27a2adf20d1c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:8","title":"Choose what varies","html":"<p>Lighting and materials change appearance; camera and base tilt change observation geometry. Dynamics such as friction or delay can also be randomized, but check which terms are active in this task. The DR label does not imply that every parameter varies.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1. Vary conditions","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"vary"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"8b981e04e1071d266b193b4a2f140765d1e9f434c15e2c270e9ea7a090b81316"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:diagram:0:0:record","title":"2. Record examples","html":"<p>Collect successful demonstrations across those resets. The recorded images, joint states, and actions become learning examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"record"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/domain-randomization.html","term":"record"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:10","title":"How variation reaches learning","html":"<p>DR collects observations and corresponding actions in changed environments. That generation path differs from applying image transforms to existing samples during training. Neither method automatically fixes incorrect joint mappings or missing recovery examples. Evaluate under consistent conditions to identify which failures actually decrease.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 3. Post-train","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"train"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"e5d7628ed2df114e039604a3423ced5e44dae30e0e87685a702534ef14fda67e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:diagram:0:0:test","title":"4. Test transfer","html":"<p>Compare policies under documented evaluation conditions. Keep the checkpoint, instruction, action horizon, and relevant starting conditions traceable.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"test"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/domain-randomization.html","term":"test"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Configured variation must reach the actual images. Inspect what changes in both cameras.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","kind":"slide","index":26}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"c75814eb0919fda15a25ae789918994b1c70b631cb31206041f9d00db8bb9654","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","index":26,"title":"Domain Randomization · Camera Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:0","title":"Variation in both cameras","html":"<p>We will check whether configured randomization reaches the actual observations in both cameras. Standard, DR, Perception-DR, and real references illustrate different visual conditions. The animated captures span multiple resets and the static references use separate layouts, so they are not pixel-aligned observations of one instant. Look for what varies and what remains fixed, then connect that to the active configuration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"test-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"986bfe3dbc491dea805fb5873432d93d4002a1f9acfd46df5d2fb2569669fb01","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:9","title":"Active parameter ranges","html":"<p>Current settings include base tilt from −3 to +6 degrees and external-camera tilt within ±5 degrees of nominal. Some camera positions and wrist calibration remain fixed. After changing ranges, inspect both views across resets for visibility and task feasibility.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 9 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","source_sha256":"61ea4a65b2c2e1ef5a11ae1f108e67efb5e8f71829cc653bbe17ce29eb4e9792"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:12","title":"From settings to actual images","html":"<p>ovrtx renders a separately loaded scene, so USD changes must reach the renderer through the workshop write helpers. Verify camera outputs after restart and warmup before concluding that recordings and policy inputs contain the change.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","text":"The implementations live in source/sim_to_real_so101/mdp/resets.py . ovrtx loads its scene separately, so visual changes","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","source_sha256":"f87540ff313e421315559173b694093dc2553f81f94ea1ee47767d39de9190f3"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have followed variation into observations. Next, trace the software layers that make this connection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","kind":"slide","index":27}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"domain-randomization-in-simulation","source_sha256":"1df8767aa18ab1aa49d6356e111018d25952777402d30796232b50009bd7adbc","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1","index":27,"title":"Workshop Architecture · Connecting the Components","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal here is to distinguish the roles of Omniverse libraries, Newton, Isaac Lab, and LeRobot. We will trace one control loop to see how configuration, sensors, reset, observations, and recording connect.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"09-code-tour.html"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:0","title":"Layers connecting collection and evaluation","html":"<p>Let us follow the system behind the screens we have used. Workshop code coordinates the task, collection, and evaluation; LeRobot supplies device interfaces, datasets, and policy tools. The bridge translates joint representations and units between them. Separating these responsibilities helps us investigate a camera problem, a command conversion problem, and a policy prediction problem at the appropriate boundary.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"5b32ba066474acd0e121665395526eab8e9be467c4769f97a8204efc3661df9f","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:workshop","title":"Workshop code","html":"<p>Workshop code connects reset, recording, and evaluation behavior. The bridge translates between systems that may name or scale the same joint differently. An error at this boundary can deliver an incorrect command even when the policy weights are functioning as intended.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"workshop"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"workshop","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"1ba0524c593f94bdaa63f534b491f8603600d6ceb8d9dbe9e473eee3bd1a5576"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:lerobot","title":"LeRobot","html":"<p>Provides teleoperator and robot interfaces, the dataset format, and policy tools. During collection the leader supplies actions; during evaluation a policy supplies them.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"lerobot"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"lerobot"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:8","title":"From scene to observation","html":"<p>Isaac Lab combines assets, actions, observations, resets, and termination into an environment. Newton updates physical state; ovstage and ovrtx produce camera images from the scene. ovstream delivers those images to the browser.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Isaac Lab","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"lab"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"369d04788281f7920fe5124ec2b42faa6cd1c3a1bf973ffb0e6e00044fdf31f7"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:usd","title":"USD assets","html":"<p>USD assets describe more than geometry and materials; they also carry joint frames and physical properties. Visual similarity does not establish equivalent contact or joint references. We need to inspect the visual and physical roles of the asset together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"usd"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"usd","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"988f7a17ce093113dd06f5a2ad23d689cb9d3c081fb686c0f90c6976bf7ec59b"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:newton","title":"Newton","html":"<p>Newton advances dynamics through the configured MJWarp solver and collision pipeline. The resulting poses support the next rendering and state observations. Its physics step must be distinguished from camera updates and policy inference timing.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"newton"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"newton","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"80a4641cf880d05c93827b8e7903ed20949d4d4d046c936d8e0403d46b337cf6"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:ov","title":"Omniverse libraries","html":"<p>ovstage manages the rendering USD scene, ovrtx renders RGB for both cameras, and ovstream streams the result to the browser. Distinguish the parts shared with Isaac Lab’s camera observations from the path used to display them.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"ov"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"ov","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"c354021cdf7b02595cb426f140eebea07d1f712953302c86ada76e2746ab289c"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:12","title":"Boundaries for diagnosis","html":"<p>Image generation, browser delivery, and dataset recording are separate stages. Diagnose the first boundary where input or output differs from expectation. Trace joint issues through the bridge and action terms, and image issues through camera, renderer, and streaming stages.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Clear diagram selection","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":""}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"41641437364964d35e812a8212dcc081259a540f7d2b4c2de70d7625d82bd208"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:13","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With the tool boundaries identified, examine the Isaac Lab environment that defines the task rules.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","kind":"slide","index":28}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"935e645474d7058ec52b38fc917de63d6b0f21b3971d85352219fc69f427a385","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2","index":28,"title":"Isaac Lab · Environments and Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:0","title":"What the environment configures","html":"<p>An Isaac Lab environment is more than a 3D scene. It defines the experiment: which objects exist, how actions are applied, what is observed, and when an episode resets. The task ID selects that configuration. Within it, a leader, state machine, or policy can supply actions, allowing collection and evaluation to share the same task definition.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"0bbf539d7ae22341365146587ca006743bbb38021378fbc18da5b5e39d437cae","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-0","title":"Articulation / robot","html":"<p>An articulation is the robot’s linked body-and-joint structure. Actuator settings govern its response to targets, so identical geometry can still produce different motion with different actuator parameters.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-0","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"71045d05b4321d4f981a9089ac1b82fa5dff7fb33aea3719a4a3f657f398c7af"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-1","title":"Assets","html":"<p>Assets include the rack, vials, lights, and materials around the robot. Distinguishing contact geometry from visual appearance makes the purpose of each randomization choice clearer.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-1","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"073d95915e95b5dcfa4e7d5613a915b3df69a6fff7739fdee175a7f50bab2d25"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-2","title":"Sensors","html":"<p>Sensors turn state into observable values such as camera images or contact measurements. Not every simulated sensor belongs in the deployed policy input; choose signals that will be available during deployment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"068532e1998bc7baf71f8909136bd3e873bcdd3a31b366acbeafd13573158bc5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-3","title":"Actions","html":"<p>An action term applies controller output as simulation commands. Joint order and scaling determine what the numbers mean, making this an important boundary to check between a policy and an actuator.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-3","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"c38d0ae4ad1acde79cd04ea995114d76bda3ea95daa021cd06ad958424ff016b"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-4","title":"Scene","html":"<p>The scene places assets and organizes independent environments. In a parallel run, resetting one environment must not corrupt another episode’s state if the collected data is to remain valid.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"0418077ad7f214f8ef06d9bb58aea60f482510e0474c4928feefb81010415794"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:11","title":"Timing and events","html":"<p>With physics dt of 1/60 second and decimation of two, each control step advances physics twice, yielding 30 Hz control. Startup, reset, and periodic events run at different times. Termination indicates an episode ending, not necessarily success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Simulation settings","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-5"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"5198c8e4446ebf517d9bc13f9ef49dbc8709f71477d89fc446c58c21e15136bb"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-6","title":"Events","html":"<p>Startup terms prepare the scene; reset terms vary a new episode; periodic terms run at configured intervals.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-6"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-6"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-7","title":"Observations","html":"<p>Observations select and group values from sensors and state. Their camera roles and joint ordering need to retain the same meaning through recording and policy use.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-7"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-7","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"6d766a829df0844e88e3f11d21cd612fbbea5c3b55d37f8f48bec59e6310c0d5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-8","title":"Terminations","html":"<p>Success, failure, and time limits determine when an episode ends. An ending alone is not evidence of success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-8"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-8"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:15","title":"Observation and learning boundaries","html":"<p>The observation manager constructs inputs from selected sensors and state; GR00T does not receive every scene variable. The presence of reward terms does not make today’s demonstration recording reward-driven reinforcement learning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Rewards","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-9"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"394adaa93d201b750eab92d2a2c57f77300e19509ad2559c21fb92d0749004bf"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-10","title":"Environment","html":"<p>The environment runs these elements in time: apply actions, advance simulation, and update observations and termination conditions. Identical scenes can define different learning problems if their step or reset rules differ.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-10"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-10","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"81682d4cabf2566b48055a278bdd503b27820fcf52ba058067cdda533d1227a0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-11","title":"Policy","html":"<p>The policy selects actions from observations. During collection, a person or scripted controller fills that role; during evaluation, trained GR00T does. This separates the action source from the rules of the environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-11"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-11","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"42a0b0122ed8cd1b6e74f7dd2ee4aaeafb406033c61d68e9b1306f1c296d4b9d"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-0","title":"Environment","html":"<p>The camera path starts from the same environment’s scene and state. Rendering the correct state is necessary for recorded images and joints to describe the same moment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-0","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"7352e5009090837017c0f4ed7bf1cf8bb7c0c0a8ad5cd9bc0d8a460368b5d699"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:24","title":"What camera settings mean","html":"<p>Camera configuration combines pose, lens, resolution, update period, and renderer. The example produces 640×480 RGB every 1/30 second. These images feed recording and policy inference as well as display, so both view and timing must agree.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Camera configuration","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-1"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"camera-sensor","source_sha256":"254f9da9238a01c4f37fe6d536f07a517337a5a1cc20cdde90f477be5adfa536"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-2","title":"ovrtx renderer","html":"<p>ovrtx renders RGB from the specified camera pose and lens. Its scene updates must follow the physics poses. If the object state changes but the image remains at an earlier position, inspect that handoff as well.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"8cac077711fcf1e0086c3f60bf3291374e24c8a5d9615476df180e086c5b95b6"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-3","title":"Camera sensor","html":"<p>The camera sensor connects the renderer to the requested view and update schedule. Matching resolution alone does not make observations equivalent; timing, lens, and pose matter too.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-3","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"fdbe446ba566dce6d51dc831bc1fbe6394c98e7bddb7e8afa282fa1099a0a9c3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-4","title":"Observations","html":"<p>The resulting images become policy observations. A healthy browser preview alone does not prove that the policy receives the same fresh frames; verify the observation path itself.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"2731d907f31341307089ee5f81a9b2dad267a71b8071aabda8ecab68587497f5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:29","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Now follow reset, observation, and recording in their temporal order within an episode.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","kind":"slide","index":29}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"a48a7b6df6f385984ef3c0b3ba60303b559f3c8849980a27ba4dbff9f914d5cf","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3","index":29,"title":"Reset, Observation, Recording","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:0","title":"Reset creates a new episode","html":"<p>Separate the start of an episode from the control loop inside it. Reset establishes a new rack and vial arrangement; step applies actions and advances time within that arrangement. Resampling object positions every step would break the manipulation sequence. Reset ranges use meters for position and radians for yaw, and apply to the environments selected for reset.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"reset-terms","source_sha256":"6f57c992263a2e4cf6f8f5571e78df6fb0a96cbe4ba719d0522a281c7741e213","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-0","title":"Reset event configuration","html":"<p>Reset event configuration specifies what placement changes at the start of an episode. The configured function and sampling ranges are applied during reset.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-1","title":"Initial reset","html":"<p>Initialize the scene before the first control step, including the configured rack and vial placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-2","title":"Environment step","html":"<p>An environment step applies actions and advances the simulation. Distinguish this repeated control step from resetting for a new episode.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-3","title":"Event manager","html":"<p>A reset event runs for the environments being reset, not on every ordinary control step.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-4","title":"Randomize placement","html":"<p>The reset function samples rack placement using configured ranges. Here x and y are in meters and yaw is in radians.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:12","title":"From contact to grasp signal","html":"<p>Contact force and vial height form the vial_grasped signal. This controller heuristic serves a different role from GR00T’s RGB and joint inputs. A true flag does not establish stable grasping or completed rack placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"grasp-observation","text":"Observation terms can turn sensor readings into useful task signals. The workshop combines contact force with vial heigh","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"grasp-observation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"grasp-observation","source_sha256":"463beb78e9853351b3c5156009b0caadbddfe856f0034f7a52e13195ecbf14da"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-0","title":"Observation configuration","html":"<p>Observation configuration defines terms and their groups. The inputs used to estimate grasp status here serve a different purpose from GR00T’s RGB-and-joint inputs.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-0","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"9bb2a5d6790e57ac741521b4259b4ac1c73fb8f14e1f983a45ea1396107b6ba7"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-1","title":"Contact sensor","html":"<p>Contact readings provide evidence for the grasp check. Contact alone is not a successful placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-2","title":"Environment step","html":"<p>The environment step updates the scene and sensors. Observations read that updated state to provide information for the next decision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-3","title":"Observation manager","html":"<p>Compute configured observation terms from the updated scene and sensor data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-4","title":"Grasp check","html":"<p>Contact force and vial height support a grasp flag used while contact persists. This is a controller heuristic: evidence of lifting is not by itself evidence of a stable grasp or successful rack placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"ddf27c26f7924e21a9101196f4fcf0c7387ddfe6a9429de08c96335df596d4ef"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-5","title":"Observations","html":"<p>vial_grasped is the computed grasp indicator. This flag alone does not establish successful rack placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-5"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:23","title":"Recorder order and units","html":"<p>The loop reads leader input, converts it through the bridge, and records current images and state with the pending action. Physics and rendering then produce the next observation. Distinguish simulation radians from dataset arm degrees and gripper percentages to preserve motion meaning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"lerobot-recorder","text":"During teleoperation, the leader supplies the action. Record the current images and joint state with that action before ","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"lerobot-recorder","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"lerobot-recorder","source_sha256":"7fb64e194798ab9d68b0fdef13d164a78bd571ab77f097c40f5f33f8e8fda3f1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-0","title":"Real leader arm","html":"<p>The leader arm supplies the human-intended joint commands. Pair these inputs with the current simulation observations to record demonstrations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-1","title":"lerobot_interface","html":"<p>The bridge reads the leader and converts its values into the simulation action order and units. The mapping must remain consistent on the recording side too, including degrees versus radians and the gripper representation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-1","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"e4407cc21484c5cad2d9f2f0facef61b55734072c80366eb525de85b2dea6406"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-2","title":"lerobot_agent","html":"<p>Read the action, record it with the current observation, then advance simulation. The rate limiter paces the next iteration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-3","title":"Environment","html":"<p>The environment applies the command and produces the next state and camera images. These become the following iteration’s observations. Preserve that ordering rather than accidentally mixing a previous command with a later state as a training pair.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-3","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"f8eddf68979ca8fa8401fc5a7a243632fbae5844c9d12b23c9415765c33f259e"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-4","title":"LeRobot recorder","html":"<p>Record the current joint state and camera images together with the action applied next, before advancing simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-5","title":"LeRobot dataset","html":"<p>Save synchronized observations and actions as an episode for later review and training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-5"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:36","title":"What changes during evaluation","html":"<p>Evaluation replaces the leader with the policy. The timing relationship remains the same: the current observation informs the next action. This shared path connects recorded demonstrations with policy execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"follow-one-control-step","text":"During policy evaluation, the policy supplies actions instead of the leader. The same simulation scene and task checks t","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"follow-one-control-step","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"follow-one-control-step","source_sha256":"af39bbd09610af9434f61d0680d3cc7dea7ede27baa6e6804c841c551476f68c"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:37","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>This ordering makes human and automatic-controller commands compatible learning examples. After the break, we will explore automatic collection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","kind":"slide","index":30}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"reset-terms","source_sha256":"3e0df9acd5fed702f24e75766df16fa5005ba92d9a0efae1d6554e8ba7d23f62","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"qa-1150--start","index":30,"title":"Break","steps":[{"id":"qa-1150--start:cue:0","title":"Break","html":"<p>We have traced how a human command becomes a recorded training example. Let us take a break. When we return, we will turn demonstrated motions into rules that can generate examples across different layouts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1150--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"a5155db27472433a9565e524acaf9f98a7cd7c8a072675be5d392448a3a81c59","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"qa-1150--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Welcome back. Keep the recording path while replacing repeated manual operation with explicit motion rules.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1150--start","kind":"slide","index":31}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"2660f17e25922dadc430798ef1a0e541b1756f4f91b61d7de21aa3fefe79563d","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","index":31,"title":"From Demonstrations to a State Machine","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will examine how real demonstrations become motion phases and transition conditions. By comparing human and generated trajectories and timing, we will identify changes to request from an agent or controller before collecting a larger dataset.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:0","title":"Extract motion rules","html":"<p>To scale a demonstration, separate the motion stages from the conditions for moving between them: approach, close the gripper, confirm the grasp, then lift. A state machine makes those phases and transitions explicit. Its targets must adapt to the object arrangement; copying one successful joint trajectory would not provide that adaptation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"from-a-few-demonstrations-to-new-layouts","source_sha256":"837df68f00fd419dd1373efae4985cb2e423e8ab8cc8bdc3538c65962a663295","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:7","title":"Implement with matched conditions","html":"<p>This example starts from ten real demonstrations. The agent analyzes trajectories, speeds, and grasp timing without changing the scene to make success easier. Compare the generated controller under matched scene and camera conditions to assess motion improvements.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","text":"We seed the process with 10 real demonstrations from sreetz-nv/so101_real_manual . The prompt keeps the agent focused on","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","source_sha256":"c1636f8f12ec06db37daf7ecc68cc886dd42d6b92143ddcea5152667de6ccbbf"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:9","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Task completion alone is not enough. Compare generated motion with human demonstrations as future training examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","kind":"slide","index":32}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"from-a-few-demonstrations-to-new-layouts","source_sha256":"862ac55e182474c2cae7bd460bd42227de715ee1cf8d35dfdb05e4ffd1a47289","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","index":32,"title":"Analyzing and Improving Motion","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:0","title":"From success to motion quality","html":"<p>Once an automatic controller completes the task, ask how it succeeded. Its approach path, grasp height, and pauses all become part of the policy’s training examples. Repeated unnecessary hesitation is data too. We will align human and generated motions at an event such as gripper closure and compare what happens before and after it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"analyze-the-motion-then-refine-it","source_sha256":"4979a654440e283d694844c0d82b3cd9f658e57b5e2484769376b6325cfe529e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:video:0:1","title":"Compare demonstrated and generated motion","html":"<p>Compare the real demonstration with the baseline and refined state machines. Watch the approach path and when lifting begins after gripper closure to identify the motion changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:7","title":"Pose and visual input","html":"<p>Varied object positions do not broaden state coverage if the arm always starts in one pose. Grasp height also changes how the cap and body appear in the wrist image. Compare starting and grasp poses separately.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","text":"A state machine that always starts in one pose can produce a narrow training distribution, even when the vial and rack p","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","source_sha256":"738088630d2fd4e28db589cadda5027b83a2a9227d4c24509e1b75654e9773fa"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:13","title":"Why timing matters","html":"<p>Aligning traces at gripper closure reveals when lifting starts. Repeated unnecessary pauses accumulate stationary targets for similar observations. In new layouts, verify that motion adapts to object relationships instead of replaying a fixed trajectory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 4","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"lessons-learned","source_sha256":"2b64f2ea232e35a63dcdb157f20649870d7e087109de9a0023674cfc0bfd4418"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Apply these motion-review criteria to other collection methods to identify what needs improvement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","kind":"slide","index":33}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"analyze-the-motion-then-refine-it","source_sha256":"4c568126002b97aad99feba5fcdb79b5cddea10401af3e970e7fb6f9cc50d7fb","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","index":33,"title":"Expanding Demonstrations · AutoData and Agents","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:0","title":"Three ways to scale data","html":"<p>These collection methods store task knowledge in different places. A state machine puts motion rules in code. Isaac AutoData adapts and recombines annotated, object-relative demonstration segments. An agent acting as the policy selects targets from its current observations and prompt. All three need motion review, but the thing we improve differs: rules, demonstration segments, or decision context.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-isaac-autodata","source_sha256":"2c5337e88475818f009f5308a550758c2dda7beca4fc9d726d08fcd2d55e397a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:8","title":"One agent decision","html":"<p>This agent receives two images, joint state, instructions, and demonstration context, excluding evaluator success and timeout judgments. Local simulation pauses during remote inference. A local adapter validates, interpolates, and converts returned targets before execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1. Pause and observe","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"observe"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-an-agent-as-the-policy","source_sha256":"54c70320bcab9535b6a747c355964c24173af0da949bce401036559e568b65d7"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:prompt","title":"2. Build the prompt","html":"<p>Alongside the current observation, the prompt includes the task instruction, a recorded demonstration, and the previous decision context. The demonstration supplies camera frames, joints, and example targets; the previous context contains the last observation, chosen actions, and a short plan note. This supplies examples through the prompt rather than updating the model’s weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"prompt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"prompt","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"e6a94a92bdd56d95ab53859d9954c152066a9d2e9def7e404e9c862ff3b64d97"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:ask","title":"3. Ask the agent","html":"<p>The prompt goes to a remote model service as a single request. The simulator stays paused until the answer comes back, so slow inference never turns into extra motion in the scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"ask"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"ask"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:adapt","title":"4. Check and adapt","html":"<p>Before anything moves, the local runtime parses the reply and validates it. The action adapter then interpolates the targets and converts them into simulator units.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"adapt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"adapt"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:12","title":"Motion quality and collection speed","html":"<p>The system observes again after up to H targets. Shorter chunks increase correction opportunities and remote calls. Demonstration context does not update model weights, and simulated motion time differs from collection wall time including response waits.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 5. Execute, then repeat","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"execute"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-an-agent-as-the-policy","source_sha256":"8d46f08b9aff460983343c0355f279efddb9d63a51430d920e9592a4096f4afe"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:video:1:1","title":"Inspect the agent rollout","html":"<p>Now watch the agent’s motion in the external and wrist views. Follow the approach and grasp as observation, target selection, and execution repeat. Distinguish motion time in the video from collection time that includes remote-response waits.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Whichever collection method we use, learned behavior needs evaluation. Test a prepared checkpoint on the task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","kind":"slide","index":34}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-isaac-autodata","source_sha256":"ec967b0ef838a2806be764637a620f56970fb0f9a551e2b1ca89eaf7c33720f6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","index":34,"title":"Sim Evaluation · Execution and Comparison Conditions","steps":[{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to run simulation evaluation and compare baseline and domain-randomized conditions. We will check the checkpoint and starting conditions, record grasp, placement, and failure stages, and keep these results separate from real-world evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:0","title":"The evaluation setup","html":"<p>After examining how data is generated, we turn to the behavior of a trained policy. The policy server predicts actions from observations; lerobot_eval advances the environment and records outcomes. This separation helps us distinguish inference behavior from environment execution. Stop the previous client, confirm server readiness, and record the baseline conditions before evaluating.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"95bfd42844322150cd8c22446dbac28f56fd076f68a7d883311367412c0dd692","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:19","title":"Establish a baseline","html":"<p>Record the model, task, two cameras, instruction, seed, episode count, and 16-action horizon for the first ten episodes. The baseline’s default starting pose lies outside the training distribution, so failures do not by themselves establish poor weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"53abff4cd6273dc33642937440600e32cf9f6272e2ace8925eb27401f6b257cd"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:30","title":"Change the starting pose","html":"<p>Next add start_pose_dataset under otherwise matched conditions. It applies mean episode-start joint positions, including the gripper, rather than sampling a pose per trial. Check printed means and standard deviations and the applied pose, then compare approach and success counts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"compare-a-dataset-derived-starting-pose","source_sha256":"97bf40c98a5c632baba0a06c3bbbd7df24367be3c71e218d3d78865b3ea3b0bc"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:25","title":"What the demonstrations end with","html":"<p>Baseline demonstrations end at release, so the policy may not return home. Learned return motion differs from a simulator reset. Repeated operation requires demonstrations that include returning to a ready pose after placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","text":"The state-machine demonstrations used for this model ended without returning home, so they did not teach the policy that","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","source_sha256":"0bf7edea0c6e014ff422c5e48d5acf4e75dd92fd7adb1be08718115922b8c6c1"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:34","title":"Display versus policy inputs","html":"<p>Headless omits browser display while retaining cameras all and rendering. Camera renaming aligns simulation keys to checkpoint keys; it does not correct framing or calibration. To test DR, change the task and active randomization rather than merely toggling display.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"evaluate-without-a-display","source_sha256":"8c08f46213c934eb4508bb7c16a5fea3bda4f70976500abd27b262bf2f9b3295"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:39","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With conditions fixed, record success counts together with the stages that failed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","kind":"slide","index":35}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"7b8621b224adfd1c0fda50b6b5fa83a861324e1f27e6c88604f09b694c5cd050","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","index":35,"title":"Sim Evaluation · Observe, Record, and Stop","steps":[{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:0","title":"What to record","html":"<p>An evaluation needs a denominator and failure reasons as well as a success count. A missed grasp, a dropped vial, and a placement timeout suggest different next experiments. Record the checkpoint, starting conditions, and termination reasons rather than counting resets as successes. With few trials, one or two outcomes can look dramatic, so also look for repeatable patterns of failure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"watch-and-record-results","source_sha256":"e7a97090f756906dfdc1b8e6ed60646766b6e4a9b37c42330fada0e2913987a2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:6","title":"Clean up the evaluation","html":"<p>After the evaluation client finishes, stop its background server using the recorded PID and verify termination. The container and terminal may remain for the next exercise, but the previous inference process should release GPU and port resources.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 3 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"stop-evaluation","source_sha256":"39a60927ceca74ec76d98ef4eda4b84fc3d75278f7c9ad9b44c4a9149d39197d"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>These records anchor the afternoon’s physical comparison. Let us discuss the morning’s obstacles and observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","kind":"slide","index":36}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"watch-and-record-results","source_sha256":"61cbda94c3abb227eb3e039754af4040150bb8b32d43159d95b7c6822c70b552","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"qa-1235--start","index":36,"title":"Q&A","steps":[{"id":"qa-1235--start:cue:0","title":"Morning Q&A","html":"<p>This morning we established consistent device inputs, connected demonstrations to learning, and evaluated in simulation. For questions, describe what was observed, which command was produced, and what happened differently from expectation. Separating those three parts will give us a useful debugging method for the physical rollouts this afternoon.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1235--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"8f38dc079d71681cb46521c8fb8c0551972c8e229b6accb7cdacf416fe46686e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"qa-1235--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We will carry these questions into physical evaluation. After lunch, reconvene at 13:45 to check baseline conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1235--start","kind":"slide","index":37}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"165af15132962e0871df85993778dc8e33955b155b52b7052575277a93a5b4f7","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"lunch--start","index":37,"title":"Lunch","steps":[{"id":"lunch--start:cue:0","title":"Lunch","html":"<p>It is time for lunch. We will reconvene at 13:45 and carry the morning’s checkpoints and device references into physical evaluation. Please complete the instructed shutdown procedure for any active exercise before leaving your station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"lunch--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"70d3dbec26f607958cd3b7652c8770a59c9d895117aec87850827b1fcc552ce6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"lunch--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Welcome back. Reconnect the morning’s input references and evaluation results before moving to hardware.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"lunch--start","kind":"slide","index":38}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"cc9c149eeb0dc6e513eadc13fdb58d21a9e447f9b8c0ed9a207bd76b8ac1c587","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"return--start","index":38,"title":"Welcome back, recap & Q&A","steps":[{"id":"return--start:cue:0","title":"Afternoon comparison baseline","html":"<p>Recall the calibration, camera views, and simulation results established this morning. We are now changing the execution environment. If the same policy fails on hardware, we first ask where its inputs or the consequences of its commands differ. The methods introduced this afternoon are tools to choose based on that diagnosis.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"return--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"559362a881651e1f8fe52e16c9e1e51275e5b152a6cb6d86e8e50a7e252e310b","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"return--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Identify what can change during transfer so that later failures can be interpreted precisely.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"return--start","kind":"slide","index":39}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"a77985c16ac228fc1181c686f5d4045c5819f7b8ac66f595fb26e7be70339deb","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","index":39,"title":"From Simulation to the Real Robot","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will now define the goal of Sim-to-Real transfer and distinguish four categories of gap. We will connect observed failures with possible causes to explain why even precise simulation can produce different real-world behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"03-sim-to-real.html"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:0","title":"The transfer criterion","html":"<p>Sim-to-Real does not end with a high simulation score. Images and state enter the policy, commands affect the robot and objects, and that loop must complete the physical task. Even with identical weights, a change anywhere in the loop can alter the next observation and action. We therefore evaluate the same checkpoint on hardware under documented conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sim-to-real-defined","source_sha256":"f84d79cf00a950b031edafc3d668ec688c15a45aec069fcb31d1171ca34424b4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:7","title":"Interpret the video","html":"<p>This is simulation evaluation played at 10× speed. Successes and failures vary with layout and approach. Compare the stage that differs under matched conditions on hardware, rather than interpreting playback speed as control speed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show video 1","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Play","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"play"}},{"label":"Pause","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"pause"}},{"label":"Restart video","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"replay"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-in-action","source_sha256":"3649390218c095f6c8aa56c848a3af3de33c9cbf94ebfa4365ef270d54ad4a02"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:10","title":"Turn failures into hypotheses","html":"<p>Separating sensing, actuation, physics, and modeling gaps helps choose the next test. Ask whether the input looks different, the same command produces different motion, or contact and geometry differ.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","text":"The sim-to-real gap is often larger than expected.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","source_sha256":"48ad48752fabaeaaefe72e05ba8101e22aa88307b43237be3bbc57a1530278ce"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:13","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Calling a failure a Sim-to-Real gap is only a start. Locating the mismatch gives us a concrete next check.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","kind":"slide","index":40}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sim-to-real-defined","source_sha256":"a5634bb23d3618d532388f518f6f9dc76e680f140399175feb40953688f174b6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","index":40,"title":"Sim-to-Real Gap · Causes and Strategies","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:0","title":"Match the intervention to the gap","html":"<p>Before choosing a remedy, locate the mismatch. Sensing concerns what is observed; actuation concerns command tracking; physics concerns interactions such as contact; modeling concerns the structure and parameters we assumed. The causes can overlap, but these categories help organize tests. Diagnosis comes before deciding to broaden the data or fit the simulation more closely to measurements.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"26f66dd72ef848b55861d6ef167e2075789ea54ef351a3ccef918a419369ed31","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:4","title":"Sensing and actuation","html":"<p>Sensing gaps include lighting, lenses, blur, noise, and camera mounting. Actuation gaps arise when backlash, friction, actuator limits, or timing make identical commands produce different motion. Image comparison and joint-response measurement provide different evidence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Sensing: what the policy sees","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"sensing"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"feaf7535185fdf932002c248a2e4b0337c134bc578352006611da152e64646d8"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:actuation","title":"Actuation: how commands become motion","html":"<p>Friction, backlash, effort limits, and delay can change the path toward the same joint target. Comparing targets and joint readback over time provides evidence for distinguishing a tracking problem from a visual misinterpretation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"actuation"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"actuation","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"9466273031e1251ae3b2bbb7398c2cd3354be57e44df40b3451611c5e3a6cf73"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:6","title":"Contact and geometry","html":"<p>Physics gaps concern slipping and responses during grasp or rack contact. Modeling gaps concern link dimensions, joint frames, mass, and inertia. Identical angles can yield different poses under different joint zeros and frames, so align calibration references first.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Physics: how objects interact","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"physics"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"39b0b1d1a0867f0f92dc3f88cb61ed3f0db7a32599c14eb7517f9416f524f26a"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:modeling","title":"Modeling: how the scene is built","html":"<p>Link dimensions, joint frames, and camera mounts determine how angles relate to the hand and its images. A repeatable position offset is a reason to check these references early, while using measurements to establish the actual cause.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"modeling"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"modeling","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"04bdc388cf6d2ff9d0832f788590a94ad3a26d1e8f4249e2ad6f3cd1e3b93d20"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:8","title":"Choose the next experiment","html":"<p>DR and Cosmos expand variation, Co-Training adds real examples, and HG-DAgger adds corrections at failure states. SysID aligns simulated responses with measured motion. Even when combining methods, record changed conditions and compare which failures decrease.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Teach the policy to handle variation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"overcome"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"3fdd176b4018c6f08caddbd5014fde26a74f0a7677d467140c4d238fc0099db2"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:close","title":"Reduce the measured mismatch","html":"<p>Measure a specific difference and adjust the simulation model. Camera calibration addresses camera geometry; system identification addresses physical response.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"close"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"close"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Begin with camera inputs. Separate geometry and appearance when reading real and simulated views.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","kind":"slide","index":41}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"a08dff7b9054ccdf1aba241622c7f4dc3af32ba505d2b6deb4cd972bd1ffd0df","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","index":41,"title":"Perception Gap · Real / Sim Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:0","title":"Separate geometry and appearance","html":"<p>Separate geometry from appearance in a camera comparison. If rack edges or holes occupy different positions, inspect pose, lens, and layout first. If positions agree but brightness or color differs, examine lighting and image response. Overlays help reveal alignment errors; side-by-side views help compare appearance. Match the objects and poses before attributing a difference to the camera.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"9d0608f925702c45ea40cadff9978b8007a2b0c7c05afe17b473af687f303b9c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:6","title":"Adjust camera response","html":"<p>Gain, gamma, saturation, and grain adjust appearance through camera-specific profiles. Wrist blur uses a separate path, sampled at reset and held until the next reset. Display, recording, and policy use the processed output, so inspect the actual images and label consistency.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 7 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"f1a7b545b8c73dfb38aed842afeb4f375f851d2a8227ff41a3f350122b2055c6"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:16","title":"Align inputs before comparing policies","html":"<p>Similar images can still leave contact and timing differences. Before changing models, align camera mapping, joint order and units, calibration, action representation, and starting pose. Real rollouts then help relate remaining failures to policy and data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","text":"Before attributing a failure to the policy, check camera assignments, joint order and units, calibration, action convent","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","source_sha256":"c06892c333c83c21814c040a4689cc67993f3b4c696624e9f67e29ababf438e2"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:21","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Run the policy on hardware and connect observed failures to the candidate causes we have identified.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","kind":"slide","index":42}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"5d5ffc062b8b51da132c7d8bdf1cd8d78fd1d25866debfab921cca0978c3af2a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","index":42,"title":"Real Rollout · Sim-only Baseline","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will compare real-robot behavior across policies trained on different data sources and identify where behavior changes from simulation. We will also examine motion differences between state-machine and human demonstrations and repeated pauses or approaches associated with overfitting.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"12-real-evaluation.html"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:0","title":"The first real baseline","html":"<p>The first physical rollout establishes a baseline for later comparisons. This checkpoint was trained on 500 state-machine simulation demonstrations, with limited grasp and vial-placement coverage and no return-to-home examples. Observe approach, grasp, and placement separately, and record recovery or homing separately too. Repeatable behavior under known conditions is more informative than a single successful clip.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"123cb4406d55b3e4fd145c84fa97c3fbb424950daad66b0714f3d7325ba45434","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:10","title":"Configure model and inputs","html":"<p>Stop the simulation client and server, then set the real checkpoint, instruction, follower port and ID, and both cameras. This model uses wrist and external keys. Match arm degrees, gripper percentages, and 16-action return and execution lengths, with RTC compensation disabled.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 3 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-first-policy-trained-on-sim-only-data","source_sha256":"67fc8c43c5995175fe13d482ca4cd2b010c5a0f408fa5232e5dd2edc5c4f1ee9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:27","title":"Motion before pressing P","html":"<p>Before waiting for policy start, rollout moves the arm to a dataset-derived pose. Clear that path, inspect the reached pose and gripper opening, and confirm rack and pickup-area visibility. Focus the terminal and press P to begin a trial.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"12a1ae974aebde4cf16a96c4014c81a634170f205e1c650e8eda882a63844f96"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:29","title":"Score and reset safely","html":"<p>Use S and F to record success or failure, and N to discard a trial. Reposition objects only after return motion finishes and Paused appears. Exit with Q, Esc, or Ctrl+C and wait for cleanup; this strategy does not terminate automatically from duration alone.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"ed32fb62958db82911fd8c727efd86a27b026fa231b8f15888d5a94b919723d9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:36","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Keep the first result as the baseline. Compare its behavior with checkpoints trained on different experiences.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","kind":"slide","index":43}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"7cefeb1d91e59615e035e9783d3770fb96a7aaeccfb9d09cfe1827ab7b90a879","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","index":43,"title":"Real Rollout · Comparing Data Sources","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:0","title":"Experience beyond episode count","html":"<p>Now shift the question from data quantity to data content. Automatic simulation, human simulation, and real demonstrations contain different motions and situations. Human examples may include approaches or recovery behavior missing from the scripted controller. The checkpoint recipes also differ, so we will look for relationships between experience and behavior without attributing the result to dataset size alone.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"1f8b8847c74d7e5e194798edd3b5679d98a21d7d96224faf079dfe9ed5a943b4","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:18","title":"Human sim 75","html":"<p>Human simulation demonstrations include varied approaches and returns after placement. Apply this checkpoint’s starting dataset and camera settings and inspect whether the policy itself returns to ready. Distinguish that behavior from controller homing after trial completion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":5,"blocks":"5"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"f3b7d2d72d3d0995fb3526c7fc5fe9767699c1cca9162d8729c18fe6601b123d"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:40","title":"Revised state machine 125","html":"<p>The next model uses 125 simulation episodes from a state machine revised to imitate human motion. The guide reports 8 successes in 20 real trials. Starting poses, grasps, and lift timing differ despite both being automated collection; relate these to failures in your own trials.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":11,"blocks":"11"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"0b087217797b6f4e3dafa317f164b40b9202701256605eb9404b8e9495e4b292"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:62","title":"Real-only reference","html":"<p>Johnny42 is a reference trained on 50 real demonstrations. Its fixed-camera key is front rather than external, and its instruction must match. Its recipe freezes vision and language, so this is not a data-source-only experiment. Restore camera settings when switching back.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":5,"blocks":"5"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"optional-compare-johnny42-trained-on-real-data","source_sha256":"f5ca306e207392d8bdc152b022a888d2846b4104b7d8bc9b1c569fe38cab08f9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:68","title":"A consistent comparison record","html":"<p>Record starting pose and layout, first-action delay, grasp and placement success, and autonomous return consistently across trials. Finish cleanup before switching models so no competing device-control or GPU process remains.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"key-takeaways","text":"Overfitting to particular grasps and vial positions can yield high simulation evaluation success rates that do not trans","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"key-takeaways","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"key-takeaways","source_sha256":"2b6c158830888f57712ba10c4693688e80af74a64445ab17a884d405a9ab69c3"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:69","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Check execution mismatches before concluding that more data is the remedy for a behavioral difference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","kind":"slide","index":44}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"5ea4d8a492127c6cb320ae90e58a7dda85012d79fff090d16a503d4e1c531390","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","index":44,"title":"Debugging Sim-to-Real Issues","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:0","title":"Find the first mismatch","html":"<p>If the robot repeatedly misses the vial in the same direction, first explain that systematic error before collecting more data. Check calibration, mechanics, and joint mapping; compare raw camera frames with model inputs; then compare predicted commands with actual motion. If the execution layer interprets a command incorrectly, another training run may leave the cause untouched.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"97f99f3c18af22b778d65c399b880ac630a7404b7e87c38b518cd44708429a4a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:2","title":"Replay and offline inference","html":"<p>Recorded-command replay bypasses the policy to test execution; offline inference tests predictions on saved observations without moving the robot. Preserve runtime units and calibration, preprocessing and decoding. Change one condition at a time and record the result to guide the next fix.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","text":"These checks come from this workshop’s SO-101 vial-to-rack case study. They are useful hypotheses to test, but the cause","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"a9ddc7517cb39da6d26459bfe80e63eee2f5485d27a46a98f5590c915e64e4c8"}},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:4","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Once the interfaces are verified, co-training can address a shortage of real-world experience through data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","kind":"slide","index":45}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"74b821132ec4e8efa8cf8703a7c6b155abb4f05798ee511bff025cf63f09b8d5","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","index":45,"title":"Co-Training · Sim and Real Data","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>This unit aims to explain why we train with simulation and real data together. We will examine the experience each source contributes and the input and action conventions that must align.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:0","title":"Train with simulation and reality","html":"<p>If the interfaces are correct but real-world experience is missing, co-training is one option. Simulation supplies repeatable variation; real demonstrations capture physical camera, contact, and actuator behavior. Training one policy on both sources can connect those complementary experiences. Our comparison checkpoint uses 75 simulated teleoperation demonstrations and 10 real demonstrations. We will check both their compatibility and their sampling exposure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"afc178b1154b8e6054717a05d62b61a42c5adabd68b8a88ec6c1e557fc1323b2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:diagram:0:2:sources","title":"1. Review both sources","html":"<p>Simulation makes repeated collection and controlled variation easier. Real demonstrations show the cameras and robot response at a deployment station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"term":"sources"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cotraining.html","term":"sources"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:17","title":"Two checks for a mixture","html":"<p>First align camera keys and geometry, joint order, units, gripper convention, frame rate, and instruction. Then inspect sampling proportions. Longer episodes supply more windows, so the 75-to-10 episode ratio does not determine training exposure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 2. Check exposure","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"kind":"diagram","term":"exposure"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"b8819c76df3aad510b587ea9376b25d55e033359ffb089adbdcf778cd2aef5cc"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:diagram:0:2:policy","title":"3. Train one policy","html":"<p>Combining sources requires compatible meanings for inputs and targets. Matching camera names is insufficient if their views differ; the same gripper percentage can also represent different openings under different calibrations. Inspect actual episodes from both sources as well as their schemas.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"term":"policy"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cotraining.html","term":"policy","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"bf7fc43a1cabecaf712c8ab2638658ecff164503f2cbd8cc5988b5784d7558b3"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:19","title":"What to compare with the baseline","html":"<p>Ask which grasp or placement failures decrease after adding real examples. Keep simulation and real results separate and evaluate under baseline conditions. If gains are limited, inspect missing real situations rather than assuming a universally correct mixture ratio.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 4. Evaluate separately","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"kind":"diagram","term":"evaluate"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"1ee8c565198f23c1fd05e2c102229929c96dc8dd462ced9e8f2500491cbe14bc"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:22","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Now examine which stages improve when the sources are combined and which difficulties remain.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","kind":"slide","index":46}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"0ebe0e11cac681db15e3acfc4a5217357689182cd6ef1493ed8297d457d39882","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","index":46,"title":"Co-Training · Results and Demonstrations","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to deploy a co-trained policy safely on the real SO-101 and record its behavior and remaining gaps. We will first identify what to observe in the supplied results, then use those criteria in real evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:0","title":"Provided results and our evaluation","html":"<p>In these co-training examples, look beyond the grasp. Lifting a vial is not enough if its tip misses the rack slot. Ask which stage benefited from the additional real examples and which difficulty remains. The prepared videos help identify that question; our station’s result still needs trials with consistent starting conditions and scoring.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"example-results","source_sha256":"6460de940100fa2b9e2fb865a599d542f615388540f733c6575b48ef14510209","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:video:0:1","title":"Co-training with another robot appearance","html":"<p>This evaluation uses the orange SO-101. Alongside the annotated attempt outcomes, watch whether the vial tip enters the rack after grasping. Do not attribute differences from the previous video to appearance alone; match starting conditions and scoring in our evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:14","title":"Add your own real data","html":"<p>To repeat with your own data, record teleoperation into a separate real dataset. The reference collection of five new episodes differs from the provided model’s Real10. The real recorder uses Right to end a segment, Left to rerecord, and Esc to end the session; wait for encoding and saving.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"optional-collecting-real-demonstrations-with-lerobot","source_sha256":"e8d2b3f57cc6820a334ee441bb268ee8e2c2d79f32238c48d54c274f39fc71d6"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:19","title":"Inspect both sources after merging","html":"<p>Merge completed simulation and real datasets into a new output. This combines episodes without enforcing 50/50 sampling. Real episodes follow simulation episodes; inspect at least one from each source and connect their revisions and the new dataset ID to the training configuration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 5 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":3,"blocks":"3"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"optional-collecting-real-demonstrations-with-lerobot","source_sha256":"ddfaeda955bd80550236d92db5b7c976955e47586b315071bd389d05dbdc4057"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:26","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We know what to observe. Run the supplied checkpoint in our workspace and record our results.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","kind":"slide","index":47}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"example-results","source_sha256":"0f89a2053983bfcebb575ec117e0f2675a40ccc553728707f5c3d548396f35d0","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","index":47,"title":"Co-Trained Policy · Execution and Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:0","title":"The Co-Trained checkpoint","html":"<p>First establish the checkpoint’s training history. Sim75 + Real10 trains vision, freezes language, and uses a 16-action window for 20,000 updates. Four accumulations of microbatch 16 give an effective batch of 64. The guide reports 10 successes in 20 real trials. We will record grasp and placement at our station separately from that reported result.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"50cbea403c74b2ba0beab5f2d4aff8214a5e0cd3b86a7b008e1e817d8a1bc1a1","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:23","title":"Use the baseline procedure","html":"<p>Keep the workspace and camera mapping fixed and apply this model’s starting dataset. Check completed startup motion and framing before P, then record grasp, placement, and recovery. Reset only after Paused appears and finish cleanup on exit.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"a738af2057b117bbf2a36c1483785721f793d8708515b916555b7a229fa77e33"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:45","title":"Compare continued training","html":"<p>The next model adds 5,000 updates on 125 broader-layout simulation episodes after 20,000 Co-Training updates. This differs from jointly training all 210 episodes from the start; its recorded real result is 9/20. Apply the second stage’s starting data and compare layout coverage and remaining failures.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":10,"blocks":"10"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"d9b05dfc7fab13d20c249ff16e57d1d267b304b1b544a4290fb390e93260c314"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:52","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>If the policy still misses a slot, demonstrate the needed action from that state. This leads us to HG-DAgger.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","kind":"slide","index":48}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"hands-on-deploy-a-co-trained-policy","source_sha256":"21b83ef620f55168de9ebd048dfcc1bb0ec033c986cc82567c573a1a77cfe085","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","index":48,"title":"HG-DAgger · Correcting Failures","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>For HG-DAgger, we will understand why human corrections in failure states provide useful training examples. We will follow the workflow while distinguishing assisted collection from subsequent training and autonomous evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"14-hg-dagger.html"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:0","title":"The action needed at a failure state","html":"<p>After co-training, the vial tip may still get caught between rack slots. Successful demonstrations rarely visit that state, so recovery examples can be scarce. Small action errors change later observations and can carry an imitation policy into unfamiliar situations. HG-DAgger addresses this by letting a person take control and demonstrate a recovery from a state the policy actually reached.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"88e0e407cca0d5891a0e453e8fdd4b62a17f38727f46cfcb15dba49e77c1b4ef","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:7","title":"Collection and training are separate","html":"<p>A correction is a supervised action target for the current state. Collection does not update weights immediately or run online reward-based learning. Inspect corrections, train offline with the original data, and then evaluate the new checkpoint.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 2 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"8075551e8c903cb926ee45102ffaff59dc751fc0ba1111154b7961b3046cc9a0"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:attempt","title":"1. Watch the policy","html":"<p>A policy can reach states that successful demonstrations rarely show: a missed grasp, an awkward approach, or a partial lift. HG-DAgger collects a useful human action at those policy-visited states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"attempt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"attempt"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:11","title":"Transfer control safely","html":"<p>Pause the policy, align the leader to the follower, and wait for correction_ready. Clear the path because the physical leader moves under motor power. Support the arm before releasing torque, perform the correction, save it, and return control to the policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 2. Pause and align","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"align"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"eeb228a8d5b3c2203b5d9e0efc406a3108617d757c13d77cdf63326982140b35"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:correct","title":"3. Record a correction","html":"<p>Guide a useful grasp, lift, or placement and save the correction. The workshop records saved human correction segments, not the preceding autonomous attempt, pause, or alignment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"correct"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"correct"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:offline","title":"4. Post-train offline","html":"<p>Review corrections, combine them with original demonstrations, and run a separate post-training job. Keeping examples of motions that already work helps test whether the updated policy retains those skills.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"offline"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"offline"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:autonomous","title":"5. Reevaluate unaided","html":"<p>Evaluate the updated policy without human help. Assisted collection successes are not autonomous evaluation successes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"autonomous"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"autonomous"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:27","title":"Control status and evaluation meaning","html":"<p>In the recording UI, green denotes policy control and red human correction. Only human segments are saved, excluding autonomous attempts, pauses, and alignment. Assisted collection success is not autonomous success; verify improvement in intervention-free trials after training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"how-it-works","text":"Watch the policy attempt the task. Look for a repeatable failure, such as grasping without lifting.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"how-it-works","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"how-it-works","source_sha256":"e1812639090ccb2ba1621298356b3a15db2e904b69323048017ad394a47a1b97"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:video:2:1","title":"Pause and correction segment","html":"<p>Watch this shorter excerpt and distinguish the autonomous attempt, pause, and human correction. Identify which segment is retained as training data. Record assisted completion separately from autonomous success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:demonstrations","title":"Demonstrations","html":"<p>A demonstration pairs what the robot observes with the human action taken next. Several successful attempts cover a band of familiar states, rather than every situation the robot might encounter.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"demonstrations"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"demonstrations"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:drift","title":"Unfamiliar states","html":"<p>A small action error changes the next observation, and the next error can lead further from familiar states. A vial tip between rack slots needs a different action from a normal insertion. That is why corrections are collected from states the policy actually visits.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"drift"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"drift","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"43536fe7689f957c006888bddc2de2f80bd3478d1bf931380ee13f49ffec4e31"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:takeover","title":"Human takeover","html":"<p>You choose when the policy needs help. Pause, align the leader with the follower, then take control.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"takeover"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"takeover"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:correction","title":"Human correction","html":"<p>Demonstrate the recovery with the leader arm: for example, lift the vial, move its tip over an open slot, and lower it into place. Save the useful human-controlled segment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"correction"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"correction"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:handback","title":"Hand control back","html":"<p>After demonstrating a useful recovery, save it and return control to the policy. In this conceptual diagram, the green path represents a human correction. The recording UI used green for policy control and red for human control, so read the control role rather than carrying color meanings between visuals. Assisted completion and autonomous success are separate evaluation outcomes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"handback"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"handback","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"5cf6a07561cd183a99e3ac02fa2f75de495f0555aedb125c0c351de094427527"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:data","title":"Recovery examples","html":"<p>A correction pairs observations with the human’s commanded action targets. A coherent lift–realign–lower sequence can be more useful than a single frame just before success. For a repeated failure mode, collect examples at different relevant positions and examine their coverage.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"data"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"data","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"1be44d10548147e4fce58f870474307829411b69f4acbeb9cadc09efc2eaf837"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:31","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Not every moment around a takeover becomes training data. Distinguish saved correction segments from evaluation trials.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","kind":"slide","index":49}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"4eb3307fbc9743a30320da5948ce4c11b157a4f6064a980dd8412a46c9de1604","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","index":49,"title":"Corrections · What to Save and Compare","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:0","title":"The unit of correction data","html":"<p>Correction data serves a different purpose from one more complete successful episode. The supplied dataset contains 29 segments, about 118 seconds and 3,544 frames, beginning in difficult states and ending when the correction is saved. Both cameras, state, and action are still needed. Its value lies in providing a useful next action where the original data had limited coverage.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"example-dataset-just-the-corrections","source_sha256":"5ab4d01d4b29f2eb3f9d3ff61cd2115b19b93fc50380d1d9802f9d09dd8a9be7","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:6","title":"When to intervene","html":"<p>Pause before continued pushing when the vial tip lies between slots, outside the rack, or contacts without entering. Use motion in both views to assess alignment, then demonstrate recovery such as lifting, realigning, and lowering.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"when-to-take-over","text":"Look at the vial tip, not just the gripper. These external-camera frames show the start of two saved corrections:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"when-to-take-over","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"when-to-take-over","source_sha256":"17331785542c10f24d6cf5733b9eec308da49c951fb113cb60d34a76b41b563a"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:9","title":"Why short segments teach","html":"<p>The trainer samples observation/action windows, so short corrections still provide useful targets. Preserve episode boundaries so unrelated corrections are not joined. After training, compare placement improvement and retention of existing grasp and lift behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"when-to-take-over","text":"GR00T trains on observation/action windows sampled from episodes. An observation supplies the camera images, robot state","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"when-to-take-over","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"when-to-take-over","source_sha256":"fb9fb9e98744216b6ffd8b83efa7d9898201f957df451faae51a183d119134d3"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:video:2:0","title":"Compare baseline and correction-trained behavior","html":"<p>Compare the baseline and HG-DAgger policies side by side in the external view. Watch slot alignment and whether grasp and lift are retained. Verify these observations with autonomous trials under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":2,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Prepare the rest poses and control handover needed to collect useful recoveries.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","kind":"slide","index":50}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"example-dataset-just-the-corrections","source_sha256":"1072486523bdb1336883bff28185f7fff5cc362a3d14176794fc345bf6a0c81c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","index":50,"title":"HG-DAgger Preparation · Rest Pose and Collection","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>In this exercise, we will learn the sequence from leader alignment to correction collection and returning control to the policy. Our goals are safe transitions, checking what is saved, and distinguishing assisted completion from autonomous evaluation success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:0","title":"The powered leader moves","html":"<p>During correction collection, pausing the policy does not mean every arm will remain stationary. The physical leader can move under motor control during startup, alignment, reset, and normal shutdown—even with a simulated follower. Clear its path and support the arm before releasing torque. We will establish those transitions before collecting data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"prepare-the-workshop-container","source_sha256":"b4e5be097acb8befe499490bf1d211e072ac862ee67d919def8f979102c4f666","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:17","title":"Save rest poses","html":"<p>Support each arm in its safe rest pose and save the current position. The command records a normal-exit reference; it does not move the arm or release torque. Without gravity compensation, the arm may fall when G or L releases torque.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"teach-the-rest-poses","source_sha256":"e495136f47a1f1b4a9e59c39cf7dfcd3438ed5a77a13eb200e3356a8632b8e6d"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:24","title":"Run real collection","html":"<p>Specify the Sim75 + Real10 policy, verified cameras and ports/IDs, and correction dataset path. The real lerobot-rollout-dagger path loads the model directly. It uses the mean start pose from 75 simulation teleoperation episodes and opens the gripper first during startup/reset, so remove any held vial.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"start-collection","source_sha256":"45e8b7fd93c88c5b81510b13c0df9640cdac1f9fe36d7977106f73fef7e9bd4f"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:30","title":"How simulation collection differs","html":"<p>Simulation uses a dedicated policy server and the current command starts from the default task pose, unlike the real path. Control it from one browser tab, store sim and real corrections separately, and clean up both client and dedicated server afterward.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":2,"blocks":"2"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"start-collection","source_sha256":"f1f354338d25ae993e672d8cbaca4f66b5bf2bb60ec298ea963116b903648ad3"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:34","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Follow each state in order: pause, align, record, save, and return control.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","kind":"slide","index":51}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"prepare-the-workshop-container","source_sha256":"1b2970d3357fbe6b6d4397f47f8c5b8adec1d1cd8f081eb11872f56b7ade1adf","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","index":51,"title":"HG-DAgger · Align, Correct, and Return Control","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:0","title":"Take over through verified states","html":"<p>Follow the handover as a sequence of states. Start the policy with Space and press Space again when help is needed. Use Tab to align the leader to the follower and wait for correction_ready. Support the leader, then use G to release torque and begin correction recording. Waiting for alignment preserves continuity at takeover as well as safe operation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"7c328ce08c31f23910a7fce310186c8d57c95fcc55dda89eb95e428a332b9919","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:4","title":"Save and return control","html":"<p>After correction, Space holds, saves, and returns control; confirm Episode saved. X holds an unfinished correction and G resumes it. C discards; R discards and resets. Real collection has no automatic success/timeout reset, so manage objects and results while paused.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"4d1a7b404b891b415822f3a2d67a715b397bfbe007cf1f4c9a170e5cc56b0909"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:9","title":"Normal exit and torque release","html":"<p>Normal exit includes movement to the rest pose; wait for completion. L releases torque and exits without moving, so support the leader first. Leader-follows does not remove the pause, Tab alignment, and readiness checks before takeover.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"review-and-finish","text":"Save a useful correction with Space , or discard it with C , before exiting. Normal exit returns the leader to its taugh","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"review-and-finish","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"review-and-finish","source_sha256":"de91b11ce19d6812e5e5942509f6c3b457cc490e952d94896fc13e83ea24900b"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:13","title":"What enters the dataset","html":"<p>Each saved human correction is one episode. Policy rollout, pauses, alignment, and resets are excluded. Inspect the segments in the Visualizer and use distinct dataset IDs for new rounds.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"what-gets-recorded","source_sha256":"7ea834972b02fbb60d7c206c3b1922ea94d02feef366603a7e523a8d8a94e214"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:16","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Saved corrections feed a separate training run. Next, evaluate a supplied correction-trained model without assistance.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","kind":"slide","index":52}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"ebf6c1f29834f4de6d7a12800d64db2893d9e9bf78d6e82a6be1436a893d4f5e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","index":52,"title":"Autonomous Evaluation After Correction Training","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:0","title":"Where this checkpoint comes from","html":"<p>Helping the robot during collection is not the final test; improvement must be checked without assistance. The supplied checkpoint was trained from the GR00T base using Sim75, Real10, and 29 correction segments. It has not incorporated the corrections you just collected. Keep that training history—and the different lengths and roles of those 114 sequences—separate from today’s autonomous evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"evaluate-the-updated-policy","source_sha256":"2ac6c37be32ab5b79de822dfaff12f35a839a682481f0b093bb78d1c452af445","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:9","title":"Starting conditions and results","html":"<p>The recorded real result is 14/20. Because corrections start mid-task, exclude those starts from the task-start average and use simulation demonstrations instead. Verify the reached pose and compare under baseline conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"id1","text":"This model was trained on 75 simulated teleoperation demonstrations, 10 real demonstrations, and 29 human correction seg","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"id1","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"9c4b248db0aacb1a4d50780c46d4347713076c7b0ed7bdad30272f339b477f51"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:19","title":"Evaluate without intervention","html":"<p>Do not intervene in these trials. Use the same scoring procedure and record grasp, lift, and return as well as placement. Assess both reduction in targeted failures and retention of prior behavior to evaluate correction training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"8611b01c56f038a306935efb68af3a753656562d6a7e1c458bb83331bdecf5f5"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:28","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Remaining unassisted failures define the next data need. After the break, examine visual and physical-model improvements.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","kind":"slide","index":53}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"evaluate-the-updated-policy","source_sha256":"5d8c447e1f0194de6cef84dd4fa574167318bf90d42b924910c50f5274f79f81","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"qa-1540--start","index":53,"title":"Break","steps":[{"id":"qa-1540--start:cue:0","title":"Break","html":"<p>So far we have added real demonstrations and corrections to improve the available behavior examples. After the break, we will examine two other directions: Cosmos broadens visual conditions in recorded clips, while system identification uses measured motion to fit the simulation’s response.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1540--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"9f181317f2d1b46a458a8c63af43abdf0018f9cd92bd5842a5cd43ca61893930","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"qa-1540--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Welcome back. Examine whether Cosmos can vary visual conditions while preserving the demonstrated behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1540--start","kind":"slide","index":54}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"16b017c4c826c27c9c4055ef93f49d737f70d91e51a96d5dd1e98fbe369b32ae","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","index":54,"title":"Cosmos · Controlled Video Augmentation","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>We will examine how Cosmos connects reasoning, world generation, and action modeling and distinguish the role of video augmentation here. We will establish how to check task and motion preservation and assess policy benefits through separate evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:0","title":"From scene variation to video augmentation","html":"<p>This morning’s Domain Randomization changed scene conditions before recomputing physics and images. Here, Cosmos generates visual variations conditioned on recorded video. That can broaden appearance, but we must check whether the original actions remain valid labels for the generated images. GR00T 1.7 remains the robot policy; Cosmos produces candidate training videos in this workflow.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"beyond-domain-randomization-and-co-training","source_sha256":"50380c6b85c71261b8e75ec0a855e68be8b0c23dc95b953366c0e424cdb9bf44","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:background:cosmos-roles","title":"Cosmos capabilities and today’s workflow","actions":[{"label":"Show architecture","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":1,"media":0}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-background","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"a28a04462d12809ac4c339db9a5c6e8247e3191137df93645b8de3ce748dd45b"},"html":"<p>Cosmos 3 connects scene understanding, world generation, and action modeling. The text and diffusion branches in this figure illustrate processing across modalities. Forward dynamics predicts what follows an action; inverse dynamics estimates actions explaining an observed change; a policy chooses actions for a task. Those are different uses. Our workflow uses video augmentation while retaining GR00T as the robot policy.</p>"},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:12","title":"Inputs that guide generation","html":"<p>Provide the source clip, a prompt describing the desired change, and structural controls such as edges, depth, or segmentation. These guide scene structure without guaranteeing preserved object shape or contact timing. Generation runs outside the robot’s real-time control loop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","text":"Consider the video augmentation workflow below. Cosmos 3 uses a prompt and controls such as edges, blur, depth, or segme","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"how-cosmos-works","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","source_sha256":"c0cc9c40705a961cfc54b4af6bd5910064b2c75df7bffe8742469c51e704b258"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:source","title":"Start with a demonstration","html":"<p>The original video, state, action, and timestamps form the reference. Any appearance change must preserve the meaning of that behavior if the clip is to be used for training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"source"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"source","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"e5e47bd27bf4bc6389639523ce694f6169b3d0ee348f9a040f3788f187416ead"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:prompt","title":"Describe an appearance change","html":"<p>Specify the intended appearance change in the prompt. If the goal is a background variation, inspect whether robot or vial geometry changed as well. A request to preserve something is not evidence that generation preserved it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"prompt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"prompt","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"4a19c01f9be7a6496cc1f59a459244f9477f57fe620cb379135d34a3526c409f"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:control","title":"Guide the scene structure","html":"<p>Edges condition contours, depth conditions spatial structure, and segmentation distinguishes regions. These controls make the desired structure more explicit, but preservation of contact timing and small rack holes still needs inspection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"control"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"control","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"1bd7a048d0aec2be954be2b22be8ea62bd8fc7e4a0266d0d56d9a1b0ca8dcafe"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:generate","title":"Generate a candidate clip","html":"<p>This application of Cosmos 3 produces a visual variation for review. GR00T 1.7 remains the policy that predicts robot actions in this workshop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"generate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"generate"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:18","title":"Can the original actions still apply?","html":"<p>Reusing original states, actions, and timestamps requires generated video to depict the same motion. Exclude clips with altered rack-hole, vial, or gripper relationships or changed grasp timing. Broader appearance still requires separate training and evaluation to establish policy improvement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Check the original labels still fit","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"review"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","source_sha256":"0053725d00cfe0b595ca248ec2bbe5601e8ef09b2e6ab37f04cfbb6eb16ae43d"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:24","title":"Compare structural controls","html":"<p>Compare the source with edge-only, edge-plus-segmentation, and segmentation-only results. Judge preserved task details and motion rather than the amount of background change. The separate SO-101 example and next dataset use different recipes, so interpret each with its own settings.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-control-signals-shape-the-result","source_sha256":"90eadff836300465ed2ebd83775b62a43bbfe976e09d585a3bde3bde9b6b2723"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:27","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Now inspect whether the original actions remain valid labels for the generated video.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","kind":"slide","index":55}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"beyond-domain-randomization-and-co-training","source_sha256":"1853bf254fdfec4754a94767bcb00d8a4fe35dede7a8e3d0514e2cdb1c03e446","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","index":55,"title":"Cosmos Dataset · Reviewing Generated Results","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:0","title":"The generated dataset","html":"<p>This example makes label consistency concrete. Two-camera videos in 10 of 250 simulation episodes were replaced with generated clips, while state, action, and timestamps were retained. Both views must therefore describe the same grasp and placement and agree with the original command timing. We are judging whether the clips remain valid teaching examples, not just whether they look convincing.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"5425d73bb8e23d9a6135c77d1fec9db1223e72f7d55bc621daefd15cd17272ba","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:6","title":"Keep or reject examples","html":"<p>Inspect episodes over time for consistent vial caps, rack colors, gripper contact, and object geometry. Color and geometry drift are recorded in this example, and policy improvement has not been tested. Exclude changes that the original actions cannot explain.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"c6a72f4d2ccfeddbb37663dfd46028481d508a8667a439c3a0b708fc777da64e"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:12","title":"Test augmentation benefit","html":"<p>Select clips with useful visual variation and consistent labels. Evaluate policies with and without augmentation under matched conditions to determine its benefit. Today we inspect prepared results rather than generating footage at each station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","text":"Use the source and generated examples above to discuss:","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","source_sha256":"ed1d2d1f751dfa61383c8d02a93a23c8ea685073480e56632f04d5ce81265307"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Visual diversity is separate from actuator response. Next, use measured physical motion to fit the simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","kind":"slide","index":56}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"d4491e1b7423da35d3d3c362d0c04449b386fdb31acd7352e821d39b3651a590","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","index":56,"title":"SysID · Matching Simulation Through Measurement","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:objectives","title":"Learning goals for this unit","html":"<p>Our goal is to use measured robot motion to fit simulation response and compare baseline, fitted, and measured motion. We will explain why actions and initial conditions must match and distinguish actuator validation from full policy evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show learning objectives","action":{"kind":"objectives","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","open":true}}],"narration":{"mode":"learning-objectives","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:0","title":"From data coverage to physical response","html":"<p>Broader visual data does not by itself fix an arm that responds differently to the same command. System identification estimates simulation parameters from measured inputs and motion. Tracking speed, overshoot, and settling depend on properties such as stiffness, damping, effort limits, armature, and friction. Measurements help constrain a model that better explains the physical response.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"6a2d406c302d88a592592b91057b0dc651c950beb0b09f625b017934131e18e9","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:excite","title":"Use informative motions","html":"<p>A stationary pose provides little evidence for distinguishing sluggish response from friction. Motions at different speeds and directions expose differences that help constrain parameter estimates. Inputs designed to reveal those differences provide excitation for identification.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"excite"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"excite","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"046c86547e98475ed1c1dce1cda53cf226f35ea9bbd00cf21059996b7913c33b"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:8","title":"Measurement and fitting","html":"<p>Align command and readback timing, units, calibration, and initial conditions. Fit parameters on some motions, then validate lag, amplitude, and settling on held-out motions. Do not attribute all readback delay to actuator mismatch.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Record what the robot does","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"kind":"diagram","term":"measure"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"610d8d22feafb9308beb32c5debfa5ab27f04e16245b513891c46bf4d7317d80"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:fit","title":"Fit simulation parameters","html":"<p>Fit parameters such as stiffness, damping, and friction to reduce differences from measured motion. Different parameter combinations can produce similar traces, so a small error on one trajectory does not establish a unique set of physical values.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"fit"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"fit","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"4578c92d9adcf4f40fa7084a1bfe91f3152e11ee72d68e4cf43a322ad0cc11e7"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:validate","title":"Test motions excluded from fitting","html":"<p>Check lag, amplitude, and settling on motions excluded from fitting. This applies the same separation between training and evaluation evidence that we used for policies. Explaining additional motions is stronger evidence than matching one fitted trajectory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"validate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"validate","review":"lecture-flow-20261123","source_sha256":"8bd173bdeb4ec014681fa3a61e48be1e76156f5e18aa890eff7ec17550d1cafd"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:policy","title":"Evaluate the full manipulation task","html":"<p>Closer actuator tracking does not establish a better policy success rate. Pick-and-place adds sensing, contact and learned behavior to the test.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"policy"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"policy"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:14","title":"The artifact compared here","html":"<p>Anchor-Lab supplies timestamped hardware measurements; here we examine comparisons using AnchorBench’s fitted USD layer. We are not fitting a new model in this exercise. Validate improved tracking and full pick-and-place success separately.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","text":"NVIDIA Anchor-Lab is a collection of timestamped measurements from physical robot experiments. It covers SO-101 arms and","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","source_sha256":"1db53c2fd8ebf9051f1ea03691ae5b4261a348e53cf6998dba1d7b557ae37e78"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:16","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Compare default, fitted, and measured responses to the same input to see what the fitted parameters explain.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","kind":"slide","index":57}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"7270a44c856bf6d7b0ea49512a02dbc60483f3b5583153af307c7d57928ec012","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","index":57,"title":"SysID · Default, Fitted, Measured","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:0","title":"Compare three responses","html":"<p>Distinguish the command from the response in these traces. Yellow is the default simulation, red the fitted layer, and light blue hardware readback. Compare timing and settling as well as motion amplitude under the same command. After a close fit on one segment, the next question is whether the model also explains motions at other speeds.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"teleop-comparison-default-and-sysid-assets","source_sha256":"e27c684c82490f94ff1fe44d029188ad572308318207d1f32de05ce552b021d1","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:video:1:0","title":"Watch the chirp sweep","html":"<p>This clip shows a chirp sweep with gradually increasing command frequency. Watch how the arm tracks the motion as it becomes faster. Then inspect delay and amplitude differences in the response plot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:10","title":"What the chirp reveals","html":"<p>A chirp gradually increases command frequency, exposing response differences hidden at a static pose. Inspect tracking lag and amplitude at higher frequencies and verify that the fitted model also explains held-out motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 2","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"example-sysid-motion-chirp-sweep","source_sha256":"3f5dbf28908d46448357cc26b79d15fab8d27a8b748f1321f98c6704687e0ec4"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>A better response trace raises a separate question: does it help pick-and-place?</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","kind":"slide","index":58}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"teleop-comparison-default-and-sysid-assets","source_sha256":"736987963797a32888ba2861b843fedb36a711acd2a23b15007762747e22e48f","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","index":58,"title":"SysID · Comparing Performance Under Matched Conditions","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:0","title":"Connect response to task performance","html":"<p>We now test the task-level consequences of a better actuator fit. Pick-and-place depends on perception, grasp contact, and learned actions as well as joint tracking. Use paired videos to inspect approach and contact, then separate the table’s training-data profile from its evaluation profile. Those two axes distinguish changes in learning experience from changes in execution conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"workshop-comparison-sysid-and-original-actuators","source_sha256":"376a7f163733f3608a7bba04bcc39906f9892e2a46e47ea8706c10aae3b37c53","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:6","title":"Read the two table axes","html":"<p>Rows indicate the actuator profile used to generate training data; columns indicate the evaluation profile. Each cell contains 100 simulation trials with matched resets, a 16-action horizon, and a 30-second limit. Separate training-data effects from evaluation-profile changes for a fixed policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-results","source_sha256":"4ea3448f5a1bd2780c09be367563dd87c38aa3f6e66077aa440cfe2e5eb38116"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:7","title":"What these results establish","html":"<p>The SysID-data policy succeeded 96 and 86 times across the two profiles; the original-data policy succeeded 57 and 49 times. Profile changes for fixed policies yield differences of ten and eight percentage points. These simulation results require real trials before making claims about physical improvement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 2 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-results","source_sha256":"4ea3448f5a1bd2780c09be367563dd87c38aa3f6e66077aa440cfe2e5eb38116"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Choose one observed failure and use these five strategies to frame a testable next experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","kind":"slide","index":59}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"workshop-comparison-sysid-and-original-actuators","source_sha256":"98a296d209b3f924268534e63ce0d28851d899a79d68363560b4f99bc7f59e18","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1","index":59,"title":"Explore · Change One Factor at a Time","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:0","title":"Define the next question","html":"<p>Turn one observed failure into an experimental question. For example, to ask whether dimmer lighting reduces grasp success, hold the checkpoint, layout, and starting pose fixed while changing lighting. Define success before running the trial and record the outcome. Aim for a comparison that explains a difference, rather than changing several settings until one run succeeds.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"choose-an-experiment","source_sha256":"b561d41f3e13260d6b9d9176731cac8bd2dde96c8f6bec0466e95622a201c0f6","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:5","title":"Test a scene change","html":"<p>Add scene variations individually on a new branch. Preserve rack access and arm clearance, inspect both views across resets, and evaluate the same policy. Without retraining on the changed conditions, this tests the existing policy’s robustness.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","text":"What might change in the robot’s real workspace? Use the Architecture Tour and Domain Randomization to choose one variat","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","source_sha256":"7f3f6d9905e4c46e986bbb8b8c1a2dbdcabd77d19cab8ea6f9b1fa0041b78872"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:8","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Apply the same control principle to scene expansion. Separate changed and preserved conditions in the Astra example.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","kind":"slide","index":60}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"choose-an-experiment","source_sha256":"a17e0c28bb822584e377b0dd8b2d663c42ce6df85db6d8e01cfd934919e7f84e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2","index":60,"title":"Astra · Exploring a New Workspace","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:0","title":"Expand the workspace","html":"<p>We can also move beyond one variable and change the workspace itself. The Astra example expands the lightbox into a laboratory scene, but removing panels and moving a camera changes observation geometry as well as background. After generation, inspect reachability, both views, and policy inputs again. Faster environment creation still needs careful validation of behavior in the resulting scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"33f4496d85795f0d28929bafe18da15d24db7151cfc37e4b816d789cca2bf270","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:3","title":"Specify what stays fixed","html":"<p>Specify both changed scene elements and preserved robot and task conditions in the prompt. Review the result on a new branch, check cameras and access, then evaluate. Agent-generated scenes and verified policy operation are separate outcomes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 7 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"8be9381368423fbadf7ab880c060e4bdd4ba6a8facf81f01739784a4227aad27"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:7","title":"Find evidence for follow-up questions","html":"<p>Use LeRobot, Isaac Lab, and GR00T sources for implementation questions and Anchor-Lab or AnchorBench for measurements and actuator comparisons. Retain execution environment, errors, and comparison conditions so the experiment can continue after class.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"documentation","text":"LeRobot Documentation","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"documentation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"documentation","source_sha256":"4e30f2ec0581d0b10cf05bd0c435fad1cff20e19d4481853e1713472f072aa6c"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The observation, action, data, and evaluation connections remain relevant in a new workspace. Bring the five strategies together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","kind":"slide","index":61}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"c68dd7a9be2c73077567741f930e5c314e4557d869de7b1e5803697be79a0437","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1","index":61,"title":"Today's Learning · Planning the Next Experiment","steps":[{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:0","title":"Return to the workflow","html":"<p>Return to our opening question. We can now explain differences in policy behavior in terms of observations, actions, data coverage, and physical response. Today’s result is more than the number of vials placed: it is a method for locating a failure and choosing the next intervention from evidence. Let us connect the five strategies back to that method.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"the-five-strategies-we-covered","source_sha256":"f6720c052b22e4c33d972023bf5c9de1bfbf647e2fc4123e3ce2c53cd0ca4560","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:7","title":"The five strategies","html":"<p>DR broadens simulated conditions, Co-Training adds real experience, and HG-DAgger adds recovery at failure states. Cosmos generates candidate video variation; SysID aligns simulated responses with measured motion. Select methods from observed failures and evaluate under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"the-five-strategies-we-covered","source_sha256":"927c8945eff115141b4c272de2a46b020726e6c39849b8290d85b3aa0b807a7a"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:13","title":"Apply it to the next task","html":"<p>For the next task, choose one question, one changed condition, and measurable outcomes, then repeat the workflow. Feedback on where you got stuck and what helped will improve future exercises.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"id1","text":"Congratulations on finishing this course “Train an SO-101 Robot From Sim-to-Real With NVIDIA Isaac.”","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"id1","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"d63f0a40657fd6a7aa24dbb80c946eee1c07a1f7adf427d638a297464bd02106"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Discuss your next experiments, repeated failures, and candidate causes that remain unresolved.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","kind":"slide","index":62}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"learning-path-summary","source_sha256":"bc39f75fec5ad2e6adc9342959f12ea4dbe1a3e31e2061ea408cf560d3a9ff93","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"closing-qa","index":62,"title":"Q&A","steps":[{"id":"closing-qa:cue:0","title":"Q&A","html":"<p>Let us start questions from your observations today: the views and starting conditions, what the policy did, and the first point where behavior differed from expectation. We will use the same order in discussion—check the setup and observations, then decide what evidence or experiment is needed next.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"closing-qa","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"4238bb9e94af90fac6ae99ae11f85a2b121d50bc554a6a428e86c7cab67d304c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"closing-qa:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Carry today’s questions and records into the next experiment. With that, let us close the course.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"closing-qa","kind":"slide","index":63}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"295ebbe3ea3f671d4b85935c469acf25bf00bb0427cd2b640e9057df25ce442f","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"closing-end","index":63,"title":"The End","steps":[{"id":"closing-end:cue:0","title":"Closing","html":"<p>Thank you for a full day of experiments. On your next task, establish consistent observations and actions, start with a small reviewed dataset, and build a baseline evaluation. Connect observed failures to better examples and controlled comparisons. The guide and your records are there to help you begin that next experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"closing-end","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"6c06db73a6c2b647bf8fd5a4eefa0427dfad83f339f863a6dae928c0504769f8","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"closing-end:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>This concludes the main presentation. The remaining slides are references for models, commands, troubleshooting, and environment preparation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"closing-end","kind":"slide","index":64}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"35705d7417a9fe84aff0b329b798fb36efe0a9794457d2ba4964878e78f4001c","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","index":64,"title":"Datasets and Models","steps":[{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:0","title":"Model references on demand","html":"<p>The remaining slides are references for questions and independent experiments. Distinguish a base GR00T model from a task checkpoint, and check data provenance, revision, camera schema, and processors together. Weights with the same architecture may have learned different input and action conventions. A local path must point to the actual policy artifact directory, such as pretrained_model or the corresponding repository root.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"current-model-selection","source_sha256":"e81a2c1f2dc04ea3424adefcd237932df1d8974baf4d97944e4dc46b9fddd796","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:43","title":"Compatibility and evidence","html":"<p>Compatibility includes joint order and units, calibration, normalization, relative-action processing, and horizon, not just camera keys. Renaming changes names only. N1.6 artifacts and generated videos are not automatically verified N1.7 policies; distinguish reported simulation and real results.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","text":"GR00T 1.7 base model and compatible LeRobot configuration.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","source_sha256":"ab88fab52d7379376aca1320d85d1e593b812881536acc6779127a333a130675"}},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:58","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With artifacts and compatibility established, find the relevant command in Quick Reference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","kind":"slide","index":65}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"datasets-and-models","source_sha256":"eed478022383fb05f81963d29c6950ca404119ef07d514e13c0258f5ba0c433a","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1","index":65,"title":"Quick Reference","steps":[{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:0","title":"Find the right command","html":"<p>Use Quick Reference by first identifying the task and where its command runs. Build, start, and attach to the container from the host; run the device and workshop tools inside it. Replace example ports and camera indices with your device values and check dataset and model paths. This is a reference for the required step, not a script to execute from top to bottom.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"build-and-start-host","source_sha256":"c5dd4b9b2b78219fa0d2522d8ed7edd4a942c319807424fbd3c3b377affd20e9","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:31","title":"Recording and evaluation essentials","html":"<p>Browser recording uses Space to start/save, C to discard, and R to discard/reset; wait for completion. Automated collection must reach its target successes and finish export. Stop both simulation client and server, and reset real objects only after Paused and completed motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"simulation-and-recording","text":"Focus http://localhost:8090 : Space starts/saves, C discards, R discards and resets. Wait for saving to finish before ex","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"simulation-and-recording","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"simulation-and-recording","source_sha256":"5dad1b03433c513fb17599b8b930d1992d05990e47f1fdb96f1f8ae3eb906bad"}},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:64","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>If the command is appropriate but the result differs, use Troubleshooting to narrow the cause along the execution path.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","kind":"slide","index":66}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"quick-reference","source_sha256":"666fbe7f6d03e58aec5b7e687083583d2df21fa4b4e86461e4732aa064a758be","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1","index":66,"title":"Troubleshooting Guide","steps":[{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:0","title":"Diagnose by symptom","html":"<p>Troubleshooting means finding the first mismatch rather than repeating the failed command. Record the command, host or container context, and actual error, then identify the hardware, camera, policy, simulation, or dataset path involved. Start missing motor responses with power, port, and cable checks; start image problems with raw previews and device indices. Each verified boundary narrows the next test.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"hardware-issues","source_sha256":"7a0fa59169e1e09a1bb4370517498a9692f809edf224c84a01dd4793c591ec84","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:45","title":"Separate prediction from execution","html":"<p>Trace raw camera frames, actual model inputs, predicted actions, and executed motion to find the first mismatch. Offline inference tests prediction; replay tests execution. Replay does not automatically avoid collisions on the starting path, so prepare the device and workspace and supervise it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","text":"When the arm reaches where there’s no vial, find the first place the observation, prediction, and motion disagree .","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","source_sha256":"5efcf990a572e6651c8b2549392c76973d9972e807a6717b8dd05022d89071c9"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:66","title":"Check saved and running state","html":"<p>Check leftover servers for memory or port conflicts and recording/final-export completion for empty datasets. Browser keys need one focused tab. Capture logs and actual state before retrying, then change one condition at a time.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","text":"vials_state_machine --record stages successful episodes and assembles the LeRobot dataset after reaching --num_episodes ","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","source_sha256":"f5841261206cd01f18edb7389cd3568b2485a0fa4fcc3922513d73d9ca9d2277"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:72","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The next references establish a new station’s reproducibility: physical workspace first, then the software combination.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","kind":"slide","index":67}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"troubleshooting-guide","source_sha256":"a6060b34ab834c5b992f03690a3417cf2fb7b836a9f45092202df89aa70fff24","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","index":67,"title":"Building the Workspace","steps":[{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:0","title":"A reproducible workspace","html":"<p>For a new station, begin by reproducing the physical conditions behind the comparison. The lightbox, diffuse lighting, camera mounts, robot clamps, mat, vials, and rack together define the reference scene. Check the actual views rather than relying on dimensions alone. This establishes a consistent relationship between the collection environment and later evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"video-tutorial","source_sha256":"de3d31bf21cb77c4eeda37ae80971fd221a8cfe6a999a95484733cfae72988e2","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:31","title":"Check the view before mounting","html":"<p>Compare sample and live images before finalizing camera holes and mounts. Keep lighting uniform and check heat and mounting stability during long use. Clear clamps and cables from joint travel so calibration records the true range.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Open tip","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"popup","id":"tip-1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"build-the-workspace","source_sha256":"5078b1ffb8634041c6ec255b64f87de63237ca9bf5ba337ac25dcfc93c3d3e84"}},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:47","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>After matching views and working space, prepare the same code and runtime. Both references matter.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","kind":"slide","index":68}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"building-the-workspace","source_sha256":"c15eb89562f49412836fbcb5322b0c31e2deb4d664fb2b69b4f9e76ef569c93e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","index":68,"title":"Get the Code and Models","steps":[{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:0","title":"Prepare matching source and runtime","html":"<p>After the physical reference comes the software reference. Prepare the specified branch, Git LFS assets, Docker, and the NVIDIA runtime, then build the workshop image from the repository root. This reproduces the intended combination of task code and libraries. Mount outputs, datasets, and models for persistence, and verify the repository’s pinned Newton and rendering stack step by step.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"workshop-software","source_sha256":"976cc336563040e6ba07e2e89a1a7dc6019035ca4ef99d22dac252caf40abe1e","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:27","title":"Containers and persistent files","html":"<p>The --rm container is removed when the main shell exits, while mounted source and data remain on the host. Container-only installations and ordinary exports do not automatically persist to a new run. Attach to a running container or restart from the actual host checkout.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"persistent-files","text":"Edits under the mounted directories persist on the host. The --rm option removes the container when its main process exi","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"persistent-files","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"persistent-files","source_sha256":"4a347d346a713e81860c0ca0e0d87e56fc2facd783f602d5c3159b466112baca"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:33","title":"Validate execution in stages","html":"<p>Validate physics-only simulation first, stop it, and then verify both cameras and ovrtx rendering. Specify camera selection and both newton/ovrtx_renderer presets, distinguishing initial cache preparation from errors in logs. Then prepare a complete compatible N1.7 policy under models.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"check-the-simulation","text":"The first ovrtx run can take a few minutes to warm up shader caches. The interactive camera display is introduced in Tel","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"check-the-simulation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"check-the-simulation","source_sha256":"8e77b4ac5929af28f45c94ed5d4f45f62f30bff2c373cdc4fa82ca6a737ee917"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:39","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>After verifying the runtime, complete preparation with policy artifacts and starting-pose data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","kind":"slide","index":69}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"get-the-code-and-models","source_sha256":"6fecd22dd9057d2362a93f274ef02743e021f6bb2f014f62ad0667cdda397460","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}}]},{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","index":69,"title":"Workshop Preparation Reference","steps":[{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1:cue:0","title":"Final preparation","html":"<p>The final preparation check is whether the complete execution artifacts are available, not just whether a download finished. Verify weights, configuration, processors, revision, and precision settings, and confirm that the container can read them. Restart a running policy after changing its configuration. Then check the data used to establish the task’s starting pose.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"workshop-preparation.html","anchor":"clone-the-repository-and-build-the-container","source_sha256":"6243e8c7eb1931dec9a6844714135fbdf8c67371314e39f8e87648ecad4dbb24","review":"lecture-flow-20261123"}},{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1:cue:16","title":"Data for the starting pose","html":"<p>Prepare dataset metadata and joint-state data for starting-pose calculation. This serves a different purpose from a full download for video inspection. Because HG-DAgger corrections begin mid-task, use simulation teleoperation task-start examples for the starting pose.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","text":"Run this from the workshop checkout on the host. It downloads metadata and joint-state data for every dataset used by --","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"workshop-preparation.html","anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","source_sha256":"57f2b3361907c73e0152d28402185c1bb4c7f97df488ef176dfb44cc5cc9bebb"}}]}];
